\begin{abstract}
The advent of smart contracts has enabled the rapid rise of Decentralized Finance (DeFi) on the Ethereum blockchain, offering substantial rewards in financial innovation and inclusivity. However, this growth has also introduced significant security risks, including the proliferation of illicit accounts involved in fraudulent activities. Traditional detection methods are limited by the scarcity of labeled data and the evolving tactics of malicious actors. In this paper, we propose a novel \textbf{Self-learning Ensemble-based Illicit account Detection (SLEID)} framework to address these challenges. SLEID employs an Isolation Forest for initial outlier detection and a self-training mechanism to iteratively generate pseudo-labels for unlabeled accounts, thereby enhancing detection accuracy. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SLEID significantly outperforms traditional supervised approaches and recent semi-supervised models, achieving superior precision, recall, and F1-scores, particularly in detecting illicit accounts. Compared to state-of-the-art methods, our approach achieves better detection performance while reducing reliance on labeled data. The results affirm SLEID’s efficacy as a robust solution for safeguarding the DeFi ecosystem and mitigating risks posed by malicious accounts.


\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Decentralized Finance, Ethereum Blockchain, Illicit Account Detection, Self-Learning, Semi-Supervised Learning.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Ethereum, as the leading blockchain platform for smart contracts, faces increasing risks, especially with the growth of Decentralized Finance (DeFi). Its open, decentralized structure encourages innovation but also makes it an attractive target for malicious actors exploiting its vulnerabilities \cite{kaihua2021empirical}. The permissionless nature of DeFi inherently creates security challenges, as these protocols often lack the centralized oversight that might otherwise mitigate such risks \cite{schar2021decentralized}. This lack of oversight also introduces risks through interactions with illicit accounts, which adversaries can exploit by deploying contracts that appear trustworthy but contain malicious code \cite{zhou2023sok}. The U.S. Treasury's 2023 DeFi Risk Assessment \cite{us2023defi} highlights several critical concerns regarding security risks in the DeFi ecosystem. Key vulnerabilities include the lack of compliance with AML/CFT regulations by many DeFi services, which increases their attractiveness to illicit actors involved in ransomware, fraud, and other illegal activities. Specific numbers reflect the scale of these issues, with reports showing over 2,000 DeFi services worldwide holding a combined Total Value Locked (TVL) of \$39.77 billion as of December 2022, with decentralized exchanges accounting for \$15.85 billion in TVL.


Detecting malicious accounts on Ethereum is crucial because these accounts serve as the primary means for laundering funds obtained through various attacks. The anonymity and lack of robust law enforcement on Ethereum make it an attractive platform for cybercriminals to convert fraudulently obtained gains into legitimate assets. By moving funds through multiple service providers such as exchanges, mixers, and loan platforms, criminals can obscure the origins of their assets, making them difficult to trace \cite{fu2023does}. Moreover, detecting malicious accounts is also vital for preventing phishing scams, where attackers use deceptive tactics to trick users into transferring funds to fraudulent addresses. Such scams not only lead to significant financial losses for individuals but also undermine trust in the entire ecosystem, emphasizing the need for effective identification and mitigation of these malicious entities. \cite{wu2020who}


For detecting illicit accounts in cryptocurrency networks, various approaches have been explored to address the challenges posed by illegal activities such as phishing, Ponzi schemes, and money laundering.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Supervised Learning Techniques}: This approach involves using supervised learning techniques, such as the XGBoost classifier, to identify suspicious accounts based on their transaction history, focusing on training models with labeled data to improve detection accuracy \cite{farrugia2020detection} \cite{palaiokrassas2023leveraging}.
\item \textbf{Unsupervised and Rule-based Methods}:  Another method emphasizes a general framework for risk rating, using a customized suspiciousness metric, which assesses the extent of abnormal transaction behavior, along with reliability and trustiness metrics to evaluate the intentions of payers and the credibility of payees, respectively.  Using network propagation techniques, their unsupervised model can adapt flexibly to various types of fraud detection, providing a continuous risk assessment that goes beyond traditional binary classification \cite{fu2023general}.
\item \textbf{Visual Analysis and Anomaly Detection}: This framework employs a visual analysis approach, which leverages a multi-view interface to visually display suspicious account detection and transaction patterns over time. This approach integrates anomaly detection, using the Local Outlier Factor (LOF) algorithm to initially flag unusual transaction behaviors, and clustering with DBSCAN to further refine these results by identifying both high-frequency “loud” activities and more subtle, low-profile patterns. This combination supports supervisors in identifying potentially illicit activities through a user-friendly, interactive visualization that aids in human-in-the-loop decision-making \cite{zhou2024visual}.

\end{itemize}


However, these existing methods have inherent limitations. Supervised models depend heavily on labeled datasets, which are scarce in this environment, limiting their generalizability and adaptability to evolving illicit activities. Risk rating frameworks may suffer from subjective thresholds and scalability issues in large transaction networks, leading to inconsistent performance. While visual analysis enhances interpretability, it requires significant manual validation and is prone to human error.

To address these challenges, we propose an ensemble semi-supervised learning framework for identifying illicit addresses within a batch of accounts, as illustrated in Figure \ref{methodology_overview}. First, we expand the batch and construct a dataset designed to provide a feature-rich environment. We then employ Isolation Forest as an outlier detection method, as demonstrated by Ripan et al. \cite{chowdhury2021isolation}, to not only filter outliers but also select high-confidence normal accounts, ensuring improved reliability for subsequent supervised learning. Following this, the supervised ensemble model undergoes iterative training using a self-training mechanism: it generates pseudo-labels for the remaining unlabeled data, incorporating high-confidence predictions back into the training set in subsequent iterations. This approach effectively reduces reliance on scarce labeled data, allowing the model to learn from a more extensive dataset that captures nuanced behaviors present in the unlabeled accounts. 

The key contributions of our work include a selective dataset acquisition strategy that enhances the informativeness of training data through network expansion and feature-based filtering. We present an innovative application of isolation forest for generating reliable pseudo-labels from unlabeled accounts, coupled with a self-learning supervised framework that iteratively refines the model's detection capability by incorporating high-confidence predictions. This integrated approach significantly improves the detection of illicit accounts while reducing dependence on scarce labeled data.
\\
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section II, we review related works on illicit account detection in cryptocurrency networks, focusing on both supervised and semi-supervised approaches. Section III describes the dataset and the feature extraction process, detailing how we address the challenges of class imbalance. In Section IV, we present our proposed semi-supervised ensemble learning framework, explaining its components and the iterative training process. Section V discusses the experimental setup and evaluates the performance of our model in comparison to other methods. Section VI provides a discussion of our results and insights on model interpretability and self-learning iterations. Finally, Section VII concludes the paper with a summary of findings and potential future directions for research.

