\section{Related Works}
Detecting illicit accounts in cryptocurrency networks has been a focus of significant research efforts. Many existing works have centered around money laundering, not only because it is a common illicit activity but also due to its connections to and implications for other types of attacks within blockchain ecosystems. The following papers present various methodologies and frameworks that have been proposed for detecting illicit accounts and anti-money laundering (AML).

In recent advancements in AML research, various techniques have been introduced to improve detection accuracy and lower the costs of investigations. Labanca et al. \cite{labanca2022amaretto} present an active learning framework that combines both supervised and unsupervised learning methods to better identify suspicious transactions. This approach includes innovative selection strategies to target specific transactions, demonstrating enhanced performance over existing AML systems. Jensen and Iosifidis \cite{jensen2023aml} contribute an in-depth review of statistical and machine learning approaches used in banks for AML, emphasizing the importance of client risk profiling and the challenges involved in suspicious behavior flagging. Their work also highlights the promise of deep learning and synthetic data generation as tools to bolster AML detection systems. Furthermore, Wu et al. \cite{wu2024xblockflow} delve into AML on the Ethereum blockchain, focusing on asset flows following cyber heists and employing taint analysis to identify laundering tactics such as token swaps and counterfeit token creation. These methods reveal increasingly complex laundering practices that exceed traditional AML frameworks.

Transitioning into graph-based techniques, several studies have employed graph learning methods to tackle AML challenges, particularly in complex networked systems. Hyun et al. \cite{hyun2023aml} explore AML in cryptocurrency networks using a multi-relational Graph Neural Network (GNN) model, addressing significant issues like sparse node features and class imbalances. By integrating adaptive neighbor sampling and analyzing transaction attributes like amount and frequency, their model demonstrates enhanced detection performance. Similarly, Cheng et al. \cite{cheng2023group} propose a group-aware deep graph learning approach that models organized criminal behaviors through a community-centric encoder, effectively capturing gang behaviors to improve detection accuracy. In another graph-based application, Zhou et al. \cite{zhou2024visual} worked with a cryptocurrency exchange to develop a visual analysis system for AML, integrating anomaly detection within an abacus-inspired interface that supports transaction supervisors in identifying laundering tactics. Together, these graph-focused strategies underscore the effectiveness of graph learning in identifying and understanding AML in complex financial networks.


Several recent studies have focused on improving fraud detection in cryptocurrency networks using a variety of approaches. Umer et al. \cite{umer2023ensemble} proposed an ensemble deep learning model specifically for detecting fraudulent transactions on the Ethereum network. By combining Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, they achieved high performance, utilizing bagging and boosting techniques to enhance detection accuracy, with the LSTM ensemble model attaining an accuracy of 96.4%. This approach highlights the potential of deep learning models in improving fraud detection rates. Additionally, Patel et al. \cite{patel2020graph} proposed a graph-based anomaly detection framework for Ethereum, leveraging One-Class Graph Neural Network (OCGNN) methods to enhance fraud detection accuracy compared to traditional non-graph-based models, such as Isolation Forest and One-Class SVM. The model’s effectiveness in capturing inter-node relationships within Ethereum has proven particularly useful in detecting anomalies associated with smart contract vulnerabilities and DAO hack-related attacks.

Transitioning to graph-based techniques, several studies demonstrate the benefits of graph learning models for fraud detection on blockchain networks. Kanezashi et al. \cite{kanezashi2018gnn} evaluated heterogeneous Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) for detecting phishing scams and illicit activities within Ethereum. Their results demonstrated that Relational GCN (RGCN), a heterogeneous GNN, outperformed homogeneous GNNs in accuracy. Similarly, Liu et al. \cite{liu2023gtn2vec} developed GTN2vec, a graph embedding algorithm specifically aimed at money laundering detection on Ethereum. By using biased random walks and incorporating behavioral features like gas prices and transaction timestamps, the algorithm significantly improved detection rates. Sun et al. \cite{adaptive2024} introduced an Adaptive Attention-Based Graph Representation Learning (ABGRL) model for detecting phishing accounts, employing a multi-channel approach with adaptive attention that enhances feature representation of low-degree nodes, resulting in improved accuracy over traditional phishing detection models. Lastly, Li et al. \cite{li2023siege} introduced SIEGE, a Self-Supervised Incremental Deep Graph Learning model addressing scalability and data labeling challenges. By incrementally processing Ethereum transaction data and combining spatial and temporal learning tasks, SIEGE demonstrated notable improvements in phishing detection accuracy on Ethereum.



Aziz et al. have proposed several innovative machine learning and deep learning approaches aimed at improving fraud detection on the Ethereum blockchain. They introduced a Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGBM) approach, which effectively addresses the classification of transactions with limited attributes, demonstrating the high accuracy and efficiency of gradient boosting algorithms in detecting fraudulent activity \cite{aziz2022lgbm, aziz2022machine}. By further optimizing the LGBM model with Euclidean distance estimation, they achieved enhanced classification performance, outperforming models like Random Forest and XGBoost with accuracies as high as 99.17\% \cite{aziz2022machine}. Expanding on this work, Aziz et al. later developed a deep learning-based model optimized with a hybrid Genetic Algorithm-Cuckoo Search (GA-CS) technique, improving detection accuracy to 99.71\% by leveraging GA to address Cuckoo Search’s limitations. This approach outperformed traditional models such as random forest, logistic regression, and support vector classifiers, marking a significant advance in Ethereum fraud detection capabilities \cite{aziz2023modified}.

Recent works in the field have explored anomaly detection techniques without relying on graph-based methods, focusing instead on semi-supervised learning and AI integration. Sanjalawe et al. \cite{sanjalawe2023abnormal} introduced ATD-SGAN, a Semi-Supervised Generative Adversarial Network model designed to address the challenge of limited labeled data in detecting abnormal transactions on the Ethereum network. By generating synthetic data to enhance training, ATD-SGAN improves the performance of intrusion detection systems (IDS), achieving detection accuracy gains of 3.78\% to 11.05\% and significantly reducing false alarm rates to just 0.15\%. In a similar non-graph approach, Olawale and Ebadinezhad \cite{olawale2024cybersecurity} developed an anomaly detection system for the Internet of Healthcare Things (IoHT), combining AI models like Support Vector Machines (SVM) and 1D CNNs to identify anomalous behavior within IoT networks. Their model integrates blockchain technology, utilizing InterPlanetary File Systems (IPFS) to ensure data security and immutability, providing a tamper-proof validation layer for the AI models. Finally, Poursafaei et al. \cite{poursafaei2020detecting} focused on detecting malicious entities on the Ethereum blockchain by employing traditional machine learning models such as Logistic Regression, Support Vector Machines, and ensemble methods like Stacking and AdaBoost. Their model achieved high accuracy with an F1 score of 0.996, showcasing the efficacy of these methods in anomaly detection.

In contrast, graph-based techniques for anomaly detection have shown substantial promise in capturing relational data within networks. Tan et al. \cite{tan2023ethereum} developed a framework for fraud detection in Ethereum by leveraging Graph Neural Networks (GNN), integrating both network structure and transactional behavior to identify suspicious accounts. By utilizing Node2Vec for network embedding and applying a Graph Convolutional Neural Network (GCN) for classification, the model achieved a high detection accuracy of 96\%, demonstrating the effectiveness of blending behavioral and structural features. Additionally, Rabieinejad et al. \cite{rabieinejad2021deep} proposed a two-phase, deep learning-based model aimed at threat hunting on the Ethereum blockchain. This model combines a deep neural network for initial attack detection with unsupervised (K-means) and supervised learning algorithms (Decision Tree, Random Forest, and Naive Bayes) for classifying security attacks, achieving impressive accuracy levels in both detection (97.72\%) and classification (99.4\%). Together, these graph-based models highlight the value of network structure analysis in enhancing anomaly detection capabilities in blockchain ecosystems.

