The algorithm \ref{alg:expansion} presented is used for expanding a dataset of addresses by iteratively adding new addresses until the ratio of illicit accounts in the dataset drops below a certain threshold (0.01 in this case). It starts by initializing an address dataset and calculating the current illicit account ratio. Then, it acquires new layers of addresses, evaluates each one, and adds it to the dataset if the risk score is below 0.2 or if the account is involved in DeFi transactions. After each iteration, the illicit ratio is updated, and the process continues until the desired ratio is met.  The final dataset comprises 44,675 core addresses, for which all transactions have been investigated, resulting in a total of 1,888,553 accounts. This way our core addresses which comprsies of 1\% illicit accounts are not only more informative but also later on can take advantage of using unsupervised methods to further psuedo-label more illicit accounts as patterns are richer. The data was collected up until April 4, 2024.

 Figure \ref{fig:interconnected} highlights a small portion of the dataset, revealing that illicit accounts tend to form clusters or interconnections with other illicit accounts, sometimes at a distance of just one node in the graph. These tightly connected accounts are often associated with phishing activities, where multiple illicit entities collaborate or share transaction patterns. Furthermore, the figure indicates that many grey nodes, representing accounts of unknown status, are embedded within these illicit clusters. The proximity of these unknown accounts to confirmed illicit accounts suggests a high likelihood of suspicious behavior, warranting further investigation to assess their potential involvement in fraudulent activities. This interconnectedness underscores the importance of analyzing not just known illicit accounts, but also their neighbors, to uncover hidden patterns of criminal behavior within the network.



\subsection{Modeling}
To effectively analyze the Ethereum ecosystem, we adopt directed graph representations that encapsulate the complex relationships within this domain. Our exploration of various graph models, as discussed in \cite{wu2021analysis}, is driven by the need to detect anomalies efficiently. Here, we briefly discuss each model before focusing on the bipartite graph, which proved most suitable for our dataset.

\begin{itemize} 
\item \textbf{Transaction Graphs:} Nodes represent transactions, and directed edges reflect fund flows, incorporating attributes such as transaction timings, fees, amounts, and computational costs. These graphs are instrumental in identifying deviations in transaction patterns and costs.

\item \textbf{Address Graphs:} These graphs feature addresses as nodes, with transactions between addresses forming the directed edges. By mapping address interactions, we enhance our ability to spot anomalous behaviors linked to specific addresses.

\item \textbf{Cluster Graphs:} In these graphs, clusters of addresses serve as nodes. Directed edges between clusters help in detecting unusual transaction flows, thus highlighting potential anomalies in broader network behaviors.

\item \textbf{Bipartite Graphs:} With nodes divided into two distinct groups—users and transactions—bipartite graphs excel in depicting interactions without overlap, thus maintaining a clear distinction between participants and their transactions.\cite{geng2021vw}
\end{itemize}

Upon comparing these models, the \textbf{bipartite graph} emerged as the optimal structure for our needs. It simplifies the complex web of transactions by clearly delineating users and transactions, aiding in both visualization and analysis.

The bipartite graph, as depicted in Fig. \ref{fig:graph_representation}, distinctively associates each user account with the transactions they partake in, thereby simplifying the detection of anomalous patterns such as a user engaging in an unusually high volume of transactions within a short time span—a potential sign of fraudulent activity. Utilizing a bipartite graph structure brings multiple advantages for achieving our goal. The explicit demarcation between user and transaction nodes significantly improves the identification of irregular behaviors, which is vital for highlighting potential fraud or manipulative actions. Moreover, this graph configuration facilitates the recognition of habitual user behaviors, enhancing the predictive accuracy of our analytical models. Furthermore, the bipartite graph enables the extraction of sophisticated features, such as node centrality and transaction clustering, which are essential for developing robust machine learning models aimed at detecting anomalies.
