At small flow time $\sqrt{8\tau}\lesssim a$, the lattice data are contaminated
by lattice artifacts.
On the coarsest ensembles, this corresponds to $\tau < 0.039 \text{
  GeV}^{-2}$.  To obtain the
$\tau\to0$ limit we consider two fit ans\"atze:\footnote{Alternative approaches  are discussed in \cite{Suzuki:2021tlr}.}
quadratic $f_2(\tau) = \tau^2 c_2 + c_1 + c_0$ and linear-log $f_l(\tau) = \tau (c_l \log(\tau\mu^2) +  c_1) + c_0$.
Moreover, we vary the fit range and always account for the maximal variation using $\tau_\text{min} \in (0.078,0.2)\text{ GeV}^{-2}$ with $\tau_\text{max}=0.3\text{ GeV}^{-2}$, and $\tau_\text{max}\in (0.25,0.35)\text{ GeV}^{-2}$ with $\tau_\text{min} = 0.14\text{ GeV}^{-2}$ to obtain the color-shaded bands in \cref{Fig.Ds}.
Since successive data in \gf{} time tend to be highly correlated, we take the limit $\tau\to0$ by performing uncorrelated fits to the central values plus/minus their uncertainties.
For better visibility, we show the two final fit values for the four different approximations (\nlo{}, \nnlo{}, with and without \rg{} improvement) in the left panel of \cref{Fig.Ds}. 
While there is only very little difference for the \nnlo{} results with and without \rg{} running, the \nlo{} result improves substantially. The overall approach to zero becomes flatter, and the spread between our different fit ansätze reduces. 
In \cref{Fig.muDs} we show the dependence  on the $\mu_\text{\uv}$ scale. The \nnlo{} \rg{} improved results show very little variation in the $3\, \text{GeV} < \mu_\text{\uv} < 12\, \text{GeV}$ range, and even the \nlo{} predictions are consistent for $3 \, \text{GeV} < \mu_\text{\uv} \lesssim 6\, \text{GeV}$. 
For our final result we quote the full spread of all fits to the \nnlo\ improved data 
\begin{align}
\frac{m_c+m_s}{2} = 528(5)~\text{MeV} \quad \text{at }\,\mu=3\,\text{GeV},
\label{Eq.ResultDs}
\end{align}
where the uncertainty covers both statistical and systematic effects. The $\tau\to 0$ and $a\to 0$ extrapolations dominate the systematic effects. As part of our analysis, we have included the uncertainty of $a^{-1}$ and the tuning of the bare charm mass, whereas effects due to choosing the fit range to obtain $\bar R(\tau/a)$ or finite volume effects are negligible. 

