\section{Reference photodiode and ND filter calibration}
\label{sec:photodiode-calibration}

The reference Hamamatsu photodiode does not come with a reliable factory calibration. Besides this, as it was mentioned earlier, neither the beam splitter ratio nor the ND filter optical density {\it at a laser wavelength} is known with a sufficient accuracy. In order to relate a reference Hamamatsu photodiode current to a NIST traceable Newport photodiode one, in a configuration with a beam splitter and (optionally) an ND filter installed downstream of it, we performed a series of measurements in configurations shown schematically in Figure \ref{fig:5}. We considered three cases: (1) no filter installed between the beam splitter and the Newport photodiode, (2) a nominally OD3 reflective filter installed which came together with the Newport photodiode (see Section \ref{sec:dark-box}), (3) a nominally OD4 absorptive Thorlabs filter we were eventually using to perform the PDE measurements.  

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figures/photodiode-calibration-schematics.png}
\caption{Configurations used to cross-calibrate Newport and Hamamatsu photodiodes, as well as our OD4 neutral density filter.} 
\label{fig:5}
\end{figure} 

Results are presented in Figure \ref{fig:6}. The PiLas Laser was set at a 0\% tune (maximum pulse intensity). Average integrated light intensity was varied by changing a laser repetition rate between 30~kHz and 40~MHz in roughly half a decade steps (10 points on a "no filter" curve in Figure  \ref{fig:6}). Ten current measurements per point were taken, and their average and standard deviation are used in three separate $f(x) = ax+b$ straight line fits. In all three cases, we observed a perfect linearity within the measurement errors ($\chi^2$ of 5.4, 5.2, and 0.02 per 8, 6, and 2 degrees of freedom, respectively), and $b$-terms consistent with 0. 
%QUESTION: was 'b' in a "y=ax+b" fit consistent with zero?


