\caption{Currents measured by the Newport and Hamamatsu photodiodes at various PiLas laser intensities, for the configurations shown in Figure \ref{fig:5}.} 
\label{fig:6}
\end{figure} 

We used this evidence as an indirect proof that (1) Hamamatsu photodiode has a linear response over four decades in light intensity, (2) one can safely use a slope of the "no filter" straight line in Figure \ref{fig:6} to calibrate the Hamamatsu reference photodiode against the Newport one {\it for QE measurements}, (3) in a similar way, one can use a ratio of the "Our OD4 filter" and "no filter" line slopes in Figure \ref{fig:6} to cross-calibrate the Hamamatsu photodiode in a low light intensity configuration required {\it to measure the HRPPD PDE in a photoelectron counting mode}. Numerically, the slope of the "no filter" straight line was found equal to 0.8409 $\pm$ 0.0003, and the ratio of the "no filter" and "Our OD4 filter" slopes (effectively the optical density of our OD4 filter at 398.6~nm) was equal to 147420 $\pm$ 90. 

