Our theoretical results use a data structure by J{\'{a}}J{\'{a}} et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/isaac/JaJaMS04}.
This data structure occupies $\Oh(N(\log N/\log\log N)^{d-2})$ space, it can be constructed in $\Ohtilde(N)$ space and time, and it answers queries in $\Oh((\log N / \log \log N)^{d-1})$ time. 
As this data structure is complex and (as far as we know) has never been implemented, for our  implementations, we evaluated practical orthogonal range counting data structures, such as Range trees~\cite{bentley1978decomposable,DBLP:conf/focs/Lueker78}, KD trees~\cite{bentley1975multidimensional}, and  $R^*$-trees~\cite{beckmann1990r}; see Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.


