%We have previously discussed scenarios where an aster's MTs are sufficiently long to span an entire cell, leading to the centrosome's stabilization at the cell's geometric center due to force balance {\bf What do you mean stabilization???}. However, an intriguing question arises: what happens to the centrosome's position when MT lengths are shorter than the cell's dimensions? Investigating the influence of MT length on centrosome positioning \textit{in vivo} poses significant challenges, as MTs play crucial roles in various biological processes, and genetic modifications affecting MT dynamics can produce complex effects. To circumbent these issues, \textit{in vitro} studies have provided valuable insights~\cite{laan2012cortical}. Specifically, artificial centrosomal asters confined within a microchamber lined with dynein motors have shed light on aster positioning under varying conditions. In such studies, centrosomes with MTs shorter or longer than the microchamber's typical length are confined between two surfaces, with the chamber's peripheral side coated with gold serving as the anchorage points for dynein motors. 

% % So far, we have described situations where MTs are long enough to probe the entire surface of the cell, and due to force balance, the centrosome positions in the cell's geometric center. Where does a centrosome position when the MT length is shorter than the cell length? Isolating the effect of MT length on centrosome positioning \textit{in vivo} is challenging because MTs are key for many other biological processes, and genetic perturbations of MT dynamics cause mixed effects. However, \textit{in vitro} studies have successfully addressed this question by confining an artificial centrosome in a microchamber loaded with dynein motors and measuring its positioning under different conditions \cite{laan2012cortical}. In these experiments, centrosomes emanating MTs that are shorter/longer than the typical length of the chamber are confined between two surfaces in a microchamber, while the peripheral side of the chamber, coated with gold, can be decorated with dynein motors. Therefore, the force-generating units are only located on the peripheral sides of the microchamber. 

% % Motivated by these experiments, we perform simulations of the S-model where we place a centrosome inside a microchamber of size $15\times 15\times 3 \mu m$ with CFGs only on its peripheral sides (Fig.~\ref{Fig7}A). When the MT average length is comparable to microchamber length ($\bar{l}=20\mu m$), there is only one fixed point at the chamber center (Fig.~\ref{Fig7}B). However, for shorter MTs, we observe a set of fixed points that distribute around the geometric center (Fig.~\ref{Fig7}C). The fixed point in the center becomes unstable (Fig.~\ref{Fig7}C, orange) while eight new fixed points emerge (Fig.~\ref{Fig7}C red and yellow). Those fixed points closer to the corners are stable (Fig.~\ref{Fig7}C red), while those in between the corners are saddle points (Fig.~\ref{Fig7}C yellow). Previous theoretical models successfully explained the positioning of the aster in these microchambers by a balance of pulling forces from motors and pushing forces from friction of MTs with the chamber periphery \cite{laan2012cortical, ma2014general, pavin2012positioning}. Here we provide another explanation for the positioning of centrosomes in microchambers by only pulling forces and stoichiometric interaction of MTs and molecular motor. 

We conducted simulations of the S-model with a centrosome placed inside a cuboid of dimensions $15\times 15\times 3\;\mu m$ with CFGs positioned exclusively on the peripheral sides (see Fig.~\ref{Fig9}A), imitating the microchamber in the experiments \cite{laan2012cortical, ma2014general, pavin2012positioning}. For a given characteristic MT length, we compute the pulling force distribution minus the force needed to keep the centrosome at a given point inside the chamber. When the average MT length ($l_c=20\;\mu m$) is comparable to the length of the microchamber, a singular fixed point emerges at the chamber's center (Fig.~\ref{Fig9}B). For shorter MTs, a constellation of fixed points appears, distributed around the central region (Fig.~\ref{Fig9}C). The central fixed point becomes unstable, while eight additional fixed points formed, with those near the chamber's corners exhibiting stability (red in Fig.~\ref{Fig9}C), and the intermediates acting as saddle points (yellow in Fig.~\ref{Fig9}C). Previous theoretical models successfully explained the positioning of the aster in these microchambers by a balance of pulling forces from motors and pushing forces from MT growth and friction with the chamber periphery \cite{laan2012cortical, ma2014general, pavin2012positioning}. Our findings provide an alternative explanation for the positioning of centrosomes in microchambers, suggesting it results from the combined effect of pulling forces and the stoichiometric interactions between MTs and molecular motors. 


