\title{Decentralised Multi-Manager Fund Framework}
\author{Arman Abgaryan*$^{I}$, Utkarsh Sharma*$^{I, II}$, Joshua Tobkin$^I$}
\date{January 2025}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\blfootnote{*Lead co-authors and designers.}
\blfootnote{I. Supra DeFi Research}
\blfootnote{II. Dept. of Engineering Science, University of Oxford}

\begin{abstract}
    We introduce a decentralised, algorithmic framework for permissionless, multi-strategy capital allocation via tokenised, automated vaults. The system is designed to function analogously to a multi-strategy asset management company, but implemented entirely on-chain through a modular architecture comprising four interacting layers. The first, the capitalisation layer, composed of vaults that facilitate multi-asset deposits, tokenises investor participation, and specifies high level risk limits and admissible venues for deployment. The second, the strategy layer, enables the submission of strategies by human developers or autonomous agents, creating a decentralised marketplace governed by a validation mechanism incorporating adversarial and gamified elements. The third, the execution layer, operationalises strategy deployment using the host blockchain network's services. The fourth layer, the validated allocation layer, assesses and allocates capital among validated strategies, dynamically rebalancing toward those exhibiting superior risk-adjusted performance. In the framework, each admitted strategy acts as a manager for the \lq\lq fund\rq\rq, encapsulated in a smart contract vault that issues transferable \textit{V-Tokens}, conveying fractional ownership of the real-time portfolio operated by the vault. The system is designed to be open to participation by both human and AI agents, who collectively perform the roles of capital allocators, strategy developers, and validated allocators. The resulting structure is a self-regulating asset management ecosystem capable of decentralised, cooperative optimisation across traditional and digital financial domains. This framework is facilitated by a host chain network, which offers native automation and data oracle services enabling vault entities to autonomously operate on-chain, paving the way for being self sufficient in dynamic allocation of capital to a range of objectives.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
    In traditional finance, multi-strategy manager companies have become a compelling structure for active management of investor funds, involving dynamic allocation across a wide range of validated strategies, whilst simultaneously enabling specialised teams to make decisions within their individual \lq\lq pod\rq\rq. The proposed framework emulates a multi-manager architecture for decentralised finance, offering a superior alternative to traditional capital management structures dominated by centralised, single-strategy models, rigid capital silos, and inefficient or opaque validation and allocation mechanisms.\\
    \\
    The decentralised multi-manager fund framework, seeks to provide an entirely decentralised, permissionless, and modular architecture that transforms capital management from static, singular strategies into a competitive and adaptive marketplace, where diverse strategy managers (human or AI) are continuously assessed and optimally allocated capital. Notably, whilst capital allocation is primarily focused at profit maximisation, allocation may also be focused on broader objectives.\\
    \\
    The framework has the following objectives:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textit{Trust-minimised execution}. Capital management, both on-chain and off-chain, may be vulnerable to significant failures due to limited safeguards and opaque execution practices, such as reliance on manual intervention for execution or unreliable automation. We address this by embedding execution within a decentralised, automated infrastructure stack, leveraging the host blockchain network's trust-minimised Layer 1, natively-integrated automation and oracle services \cite{chakka2023dora}, bridging \cite{kate2025hyperloop}, and IntraLayer \cite{abgaryan2024intralayer} coordination, to ensure transparency and dependability. Vaults are designed with strict executional constraints and programmable parameters, defining ex ante where and how capital can be deployed, minimising discretionary control and trust assumptions by design.
        \item \textit{Strategy discovery at scale}. Current fund management ecosystems rely on a narrow set of insiders for strategy proposals, limiting competition, diversity, and adaptability to heterogeneous market conditions. We seek to maximise the search space for strategy through open, permissionless contribution and evaluation mechanisms, which we achieve through creation of a marketplace of strategies.
        \item \textit{Decentralised ownership}. Currently, governance is fragmented and lacks a native layer for expressing ownership over fund performance. We seek to tokenise and financialise ownership, enabling liquid, composable, and programmable exposure to fund-level economic outcomes.
        \item \textit{Dynamic allocation}. Currently, funds managed through vaults operate with rigid allocation logic tied to a single strategy, lacking adaptability to changing market conditions. We seek for funds to be able to define the multi-strategy search space - specifying objectives, constraints, and risk tolerance, while enabling competing managers to optimise allocation to multiple strategies within that space through continuous, performance-sensitive logic.
    \end{itemize}

    \noindent
    The framework reimagines decentralised capital management as a \lq\lq pod shop\rq\rq multi-manager ecosystem, where the quartet of interlinked and synergetic layers, unlocks the potential for a competitively self-sustaining capital management ecosystem.

    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textit{Capitalisation layer}: Automated, intention-gated vaults accept multi-asset deposits, mint fungible fund-share tokens. Within these vaults, it specifies which \lq\lq fund-manager\rq\rq allocators may deploy the capital downstream, providing a clean separation between ownership, specifying constraints within which assets are to be managed, and execution.
        \item \textit{Strategy layer}: A permissionless marketplace where strategies may even derive inputs from an AI agent, wrapped as smart contract vaults.
        \item \textit{Execution layer}: Using the host blockchain network's native price feeds, automation and cross-chain infrastructure, transactions can be efficiently executed as they are specified in the core smart contracts.
        \item \textit{Validation \& Allocation layer}: Each proposed strategy may enter an adversarial validation game (for e.g., OpenAlpha \cite{abgaryan_openalpha_2025}), undergoing predictive scoring, stake weighted challenges, audits, and prediction market voting to prove compliance with the originating intention, such that strategies that clear this gauntlet become eligible for allocation. Once a subset of validated strategies is established, they may function as capital management agents within the vault as a single vault may have multiple managers, where algorithmic allocators continuously rebalance weights across an active set of strategies according to rules pre-programmed (or voted on) in the vault contracts or specialised strategies responsible for rebalancing. Here, capital is redirected toward strategies exhibiting persistently superior risk-adjusted returns, thereby diversifying exposures and mitigating systemic concentration risk.
    \end{itemize}

    \noindent
    At its core, the framework encapsulates multiple strategies acting as managers within smart contract vaults, each vault issuing tokenised fractional ownership units, termed \textit{V-Tokens}, representing transparent, real-time exposure to actively managed strategy portfolios (using the host blockchain network's resources), and also providing proportional governance rights over the management of assets in the vault (e.g., rebalancing, how the assets are deployed, control of the assets, etc.). Each vault type is defined by an explicit parametric tuple: $V = (A, \mathbf{S}, \mathbf{O}, \mathcal{R})$, where $A$ denotes permissible assets, $\mathbf{S}$ represents vector of tuples defining the strategy IDs and the corresponding amounts (share in the fund) that can be managed by a particular strategy; $\mathbf{O}$ is a vector of tuples specifying the venues along with the list of functions or operations applicable for managing capital in each context; $\mathcal{R}$ encapsulates the rebalancing logic between strategies, based on explicit risk constraints (leverage limits, volatility thresholds, or drawdown controls) and community-driven cooperative validation. The dynamic value of the V-Token for strategy $S$ at time $t$ is calculated as follows:

    \begin{equation}
        P^{V}_t = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{m} q_{i,t} \cdot P_{i,t} - F^{V}_t}{N_t^{V}},
    \end{equation}

    \noindent
    where $q_{i,t}$ represent the quantity of holdings (or weights) for $m$-many assets $i$ in the vault at time $t$, $P_{i,t}$ are the respective asset prices, $F_t^{V}$ cumulative fees payable to strategy managers \footnote{We assume that fees are not already deducted from the vault's cash ledger as they accrue.}, and $N_t^V$ total outstanding supply of V-Tokens.\\
    \\    
    Note, that the framework's design implicitly fosters a marketplace where human or algorithmically autonomous (AI-driven) participants symmetrically compete and cooperate. This is achieved by harmonising incentives among fund managers, strategy proposers, validators, and execution agents, which ensures competitive expedience for reallocation of capitals across strategies, incentivised pressure to innovate compelling strategies, and these to be provided with best execution.

\section{Architecture}
    In the framework, capital allocation is intention-gated and competitively optimised, such that each vault captures capital owners' intentions $\mathcal{I}_V$, which encodes parametric objectives over performance metrics (e.g., stability, return, drawdown). This compels strategy proposers to submit new (or existing) candidate strategies by developing strategy contracts, potentially using the host blockchain network's automation service for triggering execution. Strategies are submitted to the marketplace, which may be linked to capital managed by specific vaults, and community validation based mechanism (e.g., OpenAlpha \cite{abgaryan_openalpha_2025}) can be used to qualify strategies for capital allocation. In the context of cross-chain and cross-system execution, each vault acts as a Universal Financial Controller \cite{abgaryan2024intralayer}, using the host blockchain network's services (including automation and cross‐chain messaging) to move assets and execute logic across multiple blockchains and systems. These controllers embed instances of vault and strategy, autonomously route funds and instructions across chains and systems, whereby on‐chain scheduling and automation handle trigger conditions, cross‐chain bridge and liquidity network effect asset transfers.\\
    \\
    At the lowest level, strategies are executed on applications (either on-chain to the host blockchain network's automation and oracle data service), with IntraLayer continuously bridging liquidity across heterogeneous blockchains, validating oracle price feeds, and routing orders to the venue offering best price–time priority, thereby allowing a single vault to aggregate fragmented liquidity and execute multi-chain strategies in a trust-minimised manner (assuming oracle integrity). Altogether, this provisions the vaults into fully digital, multi-manager hedge funds, where strategy logic, risk controls, and execution quality are enforced by smart contracts, and capital can flow seamlessly between traditional and decentralised markets without custodial friction.\\
    \\
    Framework's architecture can be broadly described with the following key components:

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Deployment: The vault manager deploys the vault contract on the host blockchain network, which is comprised of six core modules:
            \begin{itemize}
                \item \textit{Venue Registry}, lists external contracts/functions the vault may call.
                \item \textit{Strategy Registry}, stores IDs and permissions for whitelisted strategies.
                \item \textit{Share Tokenisation}, mints fungible V-tokens against deposited collateral.
                \item \textit{Governance Module}, V-token holders manage whitelists, parameters, and per-strategy caps.
                \item \textit{Accounting Module}, records NAV and performance attribution at vault and strategy level.
                \item \textit{Rebalance Engine}, enforces caps, thresholds, and delisting rules for strategies.
            \end{itemize}        
        \item Capitalisation: Investors deposit assets, and the contract issues V-tokens representing pro-rata exposure.        
        \item End-application Configuration: Governance whitelists execution venues (on-chain or via cross-chain messaging) and sets capital limits per venue.        
        \item Strategy On-boarding: Strategy proposers deploy strategies on the host blockchain network, then request listing in the Strategy Registry. Note, that whitelisted strategies cannot themselves move assets, as they only submit execution intents, which are constrained by vault rules. This paves the way for a marketplace of strategies to emerge, as candidates for whitelisting (through governance), without prohibitively lofty barriers to entry.        
        \item Community Validation and Dynamic Allocation: A decentralised layer (for e.g., OpenAlpha \cite{abgaryan_openalpha_2025}) where stakers audit strategies and end-applications, score them against the vault’s intent specification, and drive stake-weighted capital routing and real-time rebalancing.
    \end{enumerate}

