\section{Product Suite}
    We propose a modular, parametric framework for tokenised and automated vaults that supports fully decentralised, interchain execution of quantitative strategies.\\
    \\
    Simply put, the framework exposes a catalogue of strategy factories rather than a single monolithic product, where each factory is parametrised using: (i) asset universe - vaults in which the strategy will conduct asset management; (ii) strategy class - formal specification of the investment or trading algorithm; (iii) execution frequency - Cadence for rebalancing, rolls, or dynamic adjustments; and (iv) Risk envelope - bounds on leverage, and drawdown limits. These parameters can be used as levers to provision deployment and customisation of a very wide range of product variants and strategies, with minimal audit overheads.\\
    \\
    The framework supports a wide range of strategy classes that can be instantiated within vaults. These are categorised as follows:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Spot Products: These products offer direct, unlevered exposure to underlying digital assets, with a focus on capital efficiency and low-friction execution.        
            \begin{itemize}
                \item \textit{Pure Spot}: Vaults hold unlevered positions in native assets (e.g., ETH, BTC, stablecoins), suited for use cases such as swaps, and reserve management.    
                \item \textit{Staked Spot}: Deposited assets are automatically allocated to native staking or restaking protocols, optimising yield, while preserving base-asset exposure.
            \end{itemize}
        \item Linear-Derivative Products: Vaults in this category systematically deploy capital using linear instruments (e.g., futures, forwards), incorporating on-chain roll-over, collateral, and margin management mechanisms.
            \begin{itemize}
                \item \textit{Index}: These vaults tracks broad-market or thematic baskets, rebalanced on a fixed cadence to minimise tracking error.
                \item \textit{Basis Capture}: These vaults are designed to harvest funding rate differentials (e.g., through spot-perpetual arbitrage), and positions are monitored and dynamically unwound based on predefined thresholds.
            \end{itemize}
        \item \textit{Non-linear Derivative Products}: These strategies provide asymmetric exposure to underlying assets, and are aimed at more risk-seeking investors.
        \item \textit{Active Strategy Products}: Vaults in this category execute discretionary or rules-based strategies that require active monitoring and dynamic allocation.
            \begin{itemize}
                \item Arbitrage: Capital is deployed to exploit cross-market or cross-chain pricing inefficiencies, with the focus of strategies on optimising for risk-adjusted spread (measured through execution quality) to capture fragmented liquidity across trading venues.
                \item Liquidity Management: These strategies can shift liquidity across different DeFi marketplaces, providing LP exposure to those platforms.
                \item Market Makers: Strategies specialised in acting as market makers across different marketplaces and financial instruments.
                \item \textit{Structured Notes}: Creates a provision for custom payoff profiles (e.g., volatility-linked, range-bound accruals) through smart contract encoded parameters, without requiring off-chain structuring.
            \end{itemize}
        \item \textit{Thematic Initiatives}: Since vaults do not necessarily need to be used only for profit-seeking objectives, for e.g., by foundations that support the development of public goods or achieve non-commercial goals, by supporting initiatives like:  
            \begin{itemize}
                \item Grant Distribution: Strategies that automate milestone-based fund distribution to projects or individuals, conditional on predefined criteria being met.
                \item Liquidity Mining: Strategies designed not for profit, but to bootstrap liquidity in new marketplaces or protocols.
                \item Airdrop Strategies: Distribution of tokens to foster community growth, reward early adopters, or incentivize participation in governance or development.
                \item Matching Funds Vaults: Strategies that match user contributions (e.g., in quadratic funding rounds), enabling fairer public goods funding models.
                \item Bug Bounty Vaults: Strategies for distributing rewards to security researchers who responsibly disclose vulnerabilities.
                \item Sustainability and Impact Vaults: Capital deployed toward environmental or social impact initiatives, such as funding carbon offset projects or humanitarian aid programs.
                \item Protocol Insurance Funds: Vaults that accumulate reserves to insure users against smart contract failures or critical bugs in public infrastructure.
            \end{itemize}
    \end{itemize}

    \noindent
    Each vault uses the same underlying allocation, strategy proposition, validation, and execution layers, as they only need to toggle the parameters of individual layers, so new products can be rolled out with minimal audit overhead and instant infrastructural composability.

