\begin{abstract}
In this paper, a learning-based approach for optimizing downlink beamforming in continuous aperture array (CAPA) 
systems is proposed, where a MIMO scenario that both the base station (BS) and the user are equipped with CAPA is considered. As the beamforming in the CAPA system is a function that maps a coordinate on the aperture to the beamforming weight at the coordinate, a DNN called BeaINR is proposed to parameterize this function, which is called implicit neural representation (INR). We further find that the optimal beamforming function lies in the subspace of channel function, i.e., it can be expressed as a weighted integral of channel function. Based on this finding, we propose another DNN called CoefINR to learn the weighting coefficient with INR, which has lower complexity than learning the beamforming function with BeaINR. Simulation results show that the proposed INR-based methods outperform numerical baselines in both spectral efficiency (SE) and inference time, with CoefINR offering additional training efficiency.

\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Continuous aperture array, beamforming, deep learning, implicit neural representation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

%\addtolength{\topmargin}{-.6cm}
\section{Introduction}
Continuous aperture array (CAPA) systems are being investigated as a candidate solution for future high-frequency wireless communications, due to their potential to approach fundamental capacity limits~\cite{CAPA_based}. Unlike spatially discrete antenna arrays (SPDAs), CAPA systems exploit continuous current distributions over large apertures, offering greater spatial degrees of freedom and beamforming flexibility~\cite{Near_field}. Realizing these gains requires precise current distribution design. This leads to non-convex functional optimization problems, which are generally intractable with discrete methods. Since the current distribution design in CAPAs is analogous to beamforming in SPDAs, we hereafter refer to it simply as beamforming.

Several methods have been proposed to address this challenge. One common approach approximates the continuous channel and beamforming functions by a finite set of orthogonal Fourier basis functions, converting the original functional optimization into finite-dimensional parameter optimization~\cite{Wavenumber, Pattern_Division, On_the_SE}. In~\cite{Wavenumber}, this Fourier-based technique was applied to single-user multi-stream CAPA systems and later extended to multi-user uplink and downlink scenarios~\cite{Pattern_Division, On_the_SE}. However, basis truncation introduces approximation errors, sacrificing optimality. Additionally, the required number of basis functions increases rapidly with carrier frequency and aperture size, leading to high-dimensional optimization and substantial computational overhead in large-scale CAPA systems~\cite{CAPA}.


To address these limitations, direct functional-space optimization via calculus of variations (CoV) has been explored for accurate and efficient CAPA beamforming~\cite {Multi_Group_Multicast, Beamforming_Design}. In ~\cite{Multi_Group_Multicast}, an iterative CoV-based block coordinate descent (BCD) algorithm was proposed for downlink multicast scenarios, where the CAPA-based base station (BS) serves multiple single-antenna users. For the scenarios where both transmitters and receivers are equipped with CAPAs, an iterative weighted minimum mean-squared error (WMMSE) algorithm was derived based on CoV~\cite{Beamforming_Design}. Nevertheless, such iterative methods may incur substantial computational overhead in large-scale CAPA systems, due to repeated integrations and sequential~updates.  

As an alternative to traditional optimization methods, deep neural networks (DNNs) have demonstrated effectiveness in precoding design for SPDA systems~\cite{Recursive_GNNs}. In SPDAs, beamforming matrices are learned directly from finite-dimensional channel matrices. In contrast, CAPA systems involve continuous channel and beamforming functions, which are inherently infinite-dimensional and thus incompatible with standard DNN input–output formats. To address this, a recent work~\cite{Multi_User_CAPA} found finite-dimensional representations of the continuous functions for the systems with CAPA-based BSs serving single-antenna users. Yet, such representations are not readily applicable in more general cases, such as systems with CAPAs at both the transmitter and receiver, where the optimal beamforming functions cannot be easily reduced to low-dimensional forms.

Parameterizing implicitly defined, continuous, and differentiable functions with neural networks enables learning without explicitly modeling infinite-dimensional input-output mappings. Such networks are known as implicit neural representations (INRs) for such implicitly defined functions~\cite{Implicit_Neural}. In this paper, we design two INRs to model the beamforming and coefficient functions, respectively. Specifically, BeaINR is introduced to represent the continuous beamforming function, mapping spatial coordinates over the BS aperture to beamforming weights. Moreover, we observe that the optimal beamformer lies in the functional subspace spanned by the channel responses and can thus be expressed as a weighted integral of the channel function. Leveraging this structure, we introduce CoefINR, which models the coefficient function used in the integral formulation. Unlike~\cite{Multi_User_CAPA}, where only finite-dimensional coefficient vectors are learned, CoefINR captures continuous functions. Simulation results show that the INR-based approach outperforms numerical baselines in spectral efficiency (SE) and inference time, with CoefINR reducing training costs compared to~BeaINR.

\section{System Model and Problem Formulation}
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{CAPA}, we consider a downlink CAPA system where a BS services one user, with both the transmitter and receiver equipped with CAPAs. In the following, we model this system to maximize the SE by optimizing the transmit-side continuous beamforming function.

\begin{figure}%[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.3\textwidth]{CAPA.pdf}
%\vspace{-0.3cm}
\caption{Illustration of the downlink CAPA system. }  \label{CAPA}
\end{figure}

\subsection{System Model}
Denote the surfaces of the BS and user CAPAs as $\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{B}\subseteq \mathbb{R} ^{3\times 1}$ and $\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{U}\subseteq \mathbb{R} ^{3\times 1}$, respectively. The BS CAPA lies on the $xy$-plane, centered at the origin, with dimensions $L_{\mathrm{B},x}$ and $L_{\mathrm{B},y}$, and any point on its surface is denoted as $\mathbf{s}=[s_x, s_y, 0]\in\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{B}$. The user CAPA, with dimensions $L_{\mathrm{U},x}$ and $L_{\mathrm{U},y}$, is centered at $\mathbf{r}_0$ and assumed to be parallel to the BS CAPA to ensure polarization alignment for optimal signal reception. To facilitate coordinate modeling on the user aperture, a local coordinate system is established where $\mathbf{r}_0$ is the local origin, and the $x\text{-}y$ plane aligns with the BS CAPA. A point $\hat{\mathbf{r}}=[\hat{r}_x, \hat{r}_y, 0]\in\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{U}$ defined in the local coordinate system can be mapped to the global coordinate system as 
\begin{equation} \label{rotation equation}
    \mathbf{r} =\hat{\mathbf{r}} + \mathbf{r}_o,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{r}\in\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{U}$ is defined in global coordinate.

Assuming the BS simultaneously transmits $N$ independent data streams, the signal at point $\mathbf{s} \in \mathcal{S}_\mathrm{B}$ is given by $\mathrm{J}(\mathbf{s}) = \sum_{n=1}^{N} \mathrm{w}_n(\mathbf{s}) x_n$, where $x_n$ is the symbol of the $n$-th stream with $\mathbb{E}\{|x_n|^2\} = 1$, and $\mathrm{w}_n(\mathbf{s})$ denotes the corresponding beamformer. We consider uni-polarized CAPA systems, where both the BS and user apertures are polarized along the $y$-axis. Accordingly, the received signal at point $\mathbf{r} \in \mathcal{S}_\mathrm{U}$ is modeled~as
\begin{equation} \label{y}
y(\mathbf{r}) = \int_{\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{B}} \mathbf{u}^T h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s}) \mathrm{J}(\mathbf{s}) \mathbf{u} , d\mathbf{s} + n(\mathbf{r}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{u} = [0, 1, 0]^T$ is the unit polarization vector, $h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})$ is the continuous channel response (analogous to the channel matrix in SPDA systems), and $n(\mathbf{r}) \sim \mathcal{CN}(0, \sigma^2)$ denotes thermal noise.

Under the line-of-sight condition, the channel response between the BS and user CAPAs $h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})$ can be modeled as~\cite{Beamforming_Design}  
\begin{equation} \label{Green's function}
h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s}) = \frac{-j \eta e^{-j \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \| \mathbf{r} - \mathbf{s} \|}}{2\lambda \| \mathbf{r} - \mathbf{s} \|} 
\left( \mathbf{I}_3 - \frac{(\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{s})(\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{s})^T}{\| \mathbf{r} - \mathbf{s} \|^2} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\eta$ is the intrinsic impedance, and $\lambda=c/f$ is the signal wavelength, with $f$ being the carrier frequency and $c$ the speed of light.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
With the system model described above, we formulate the BS-side CAPA beamforming problem. Taking SE maximization as an example, the problem can be expressed as~\cite{Beamforming_Design}
\begin{subequations} \label{P0:Power0}
    \begin{align}
    \max_{\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s})} \quad & \log \det \left( \mathbf{I}_N + \frac{1}{\sigma^2} \mathbf{Q} \right) \label{eq:object} \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad 
    & \mathbf{Q} = \int_{\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{U}} \mathbf{e}^\mathrm{H}(\mathbf{r}) \mathbf{e}(\mathbf{r}) \, d\mathbf{r}, \label{eq:E1} \\
    & \mathbf{e}(\mathbf{r}) = \int_{\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{B}} h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s}) \mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) \, d\mathbf{s}, \label{eq:E2} \\
    & \int_{\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{B}} \| \mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) \|^2 \, d\mathbf{s} \leq P_{\max}, \label{eq:PowerConstraint}
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s})=[\mathrm{w}_1(\mathbf{s}), \cdots, \mathrm{w}_N(\mathbf{s})]\in\mathbb{C}^{1\times N}$, $\mathbf{e}(\mathbf{r})$ denotes the effective received signal at $\mathbf{r}\in\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{U}$, $\mathbf{Q}$ is the resulting signal covariance matrix, and $P_{\max}$ is the maximal transmit power.

Problem~\eqref{P0:Power0} is a functional optimization problem that cannot be handled by conventional solvers such as CVX. Moreover, both the channel and beamforming are functions that can be regarded as vectors with infinite dimensions, making them incompatible with direct input or output in standard DNNs.


\begin{proposition} \label{Proposition1}
The optimal precoding function \( \mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) \) lies in the functional subspace spanned by the user-side channel responses \( \{ h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s}) \}_{\mathbf{r} \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}}} \). That is, \( \mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) \) can be represented as
\[
\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) = \int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}}} h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})\, \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r}) \, d\mathbf{r},
\]
where \( \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r}) = [c_1(\mathbf{r}), \cdots, c_N(\mathbf{r})] \in \mathbb{C}^{1\times N} \) denotes a set of coefficient functions defined over \( \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}} \), and \( c_n(\mathbf{r}) \) corresponds to the \( n \)-th data stream.
\end{proposition}
\begin{IEEEproof}
See Appendix~\ref{appendix1}.
\end{IEEEproof}

\section{INR of Beamforming Function}
In this section, we first design an INR, termed BeaINR, to represent the continuous beamforming function over the BS aperture, followed by a customized training strategy to mitigate overfitting.

\subsection{Design of BeaINR}
The beamforming function is implicitly defined by problem~\eqref{P0:Power0}. INRs can parameterize this function by mapping spatial coordinates $\mathbf{s} \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}$ to beamformer values while satisfying the associated constraints. To this end, we introduce BeaINR, which takes $\mathbf{s}$ as input and outputs the corresponding beamforming coefficient. Since $\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s})$ depends on the channel response $h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})$, which is primarily determined by the user’s center coordinate $\mathbf{r}_o$ as indicated by the model in~\eqref{Green's function}, we adopt $\mathbf{r}_o$ as a finite-dimensional representation of the channel~\cite{Multi_User_CAPA}. To enable channel-aware adaptation, BeaINR is modeled as
\begin{equation} \label{BeaINR}
\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) = \mathcal{P}_{\theta_{\mathrm{p}}}(\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{r}_o), \quad \mathbf{s} \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{P}_{\theta_{\mathrm{p}}}(\cdot)$ denotes the INR-based beamforming function parameterized by $\theta_{\mathrm{p}}$.


\subsection{Training Method} \label{Training Method}
Supervised learning is impractical in this setting, as using spatial coordinates and their corresponding beamforming values as labels would require an infinite training set, resulting in prohibitive training complexity. Unsupervised learning of BeaINR is also nontrivial, since the intricate functional forms of both BeaINR and $h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})$ lead to intractable integrals in the objective, hindering gradient backpropagation. To address this, we adopt Gauss–Legendre (GL) quadrature to approximate the integrals. Specifically, for a continuous function $f(\cdot)$ defined over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}$, the integral is approximated as~\cite{Beamforming_Design}
\begin{align} \label{GL of S}
\int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}} f(\mathbf{s})d\mathbf{s}
\approx \sum_{n=1}^{M} \sum_{m=1}^{M} \frac{\omega_n \omega_m A_{\mathrm{B}}}{4} f(\mathbf{s}_{n,m}),\quad \mathbf{s}_{m,n} \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}},
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{s}_{m,n} = \left[ \frac{\theta_m L_{\mathrm{B},x}}{2},\, \frac{\theta_n L_{\mathrm{B},y}}{2},\, 0 \right]^T$ are the sampling points, $A_{\mathrm{B}} =   L_{\mathrm{B},x} L_{\mathrm{B},y}$ is the aperture area, $M$ is the quadrature order, and $\{\theta_m, \omega_m\}_{m=1}^{M}$ are the roots and weights of the Legendre polynomial. This quadrature reformulation converts the objective into a finite sum, enabling differentiable computation and gradient-based training. Building upon this reformulation, the detailed training procedure for BeaINR is detailed next.

Based on this, we construct a training dataset for BeaINR. Each sample consists of a fixed set of GL quadrature points and a randomly generated user position. Specifically, $M_{\mathrm{B,G}}$ GL roots and weights $\{\theta_i, \omega_i\}_{i=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B,G}}}$ are used to sample $M_{\mathrm{B,G}}^2$ spatial points over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}$, denoted by $\{\mathbf{s}_{i,j}\}_{i,j=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B,G}}}$, which are shared across all samples. The user position varies per sample and is denoted $\mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}$ for the $k$-th sample. The resulting training dataset is defined as
\begin{equation} \label{Dataset D}
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}} = \left\{ \left( \left\{ \mathbf{s}_{i,j} \right\}_{i,j=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B},\mathrm{G}}},\, \mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}\right) \right\}_{k=1}^{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}\right|},
\end{equation}
where $\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}\right|$ denotes the  number of samples. 

The loss over the dataset $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}$ is computed using GL quadrature. For the $k$-th sample, the beamforming function is obtained by inputting $\mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}$ into the BeaINR, denoted~as 
\begin{equation} \label{beamforming vector}
\!\mathbf{w}^{(k)}(\mathbf{s}) \!= \!\mathcal{P}_{\theta_{\mathrm{p}}}(\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}),\quad \mathbf{s}\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}.
\end{equation}
To satisfy the power constraint in~\eqref{eq:PowerConstraint}, the beamforming function $\mathbf{w}^{(k)}(\mathbf{s})$ for each training sample is normalized by $P_{\max}$. The effective received signal is then derived as 
\begin{equation} \label{integral of e}
\mathbf{e}^{(k)}(\mathbf{r}) \approx \sum_{i=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B},\mathrm{G}}} \sum_{j=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B},\mathrm{G}}} \frac{\omega_{i}\omega_{j}A_{\mathrm{B}}}{4} \cdot h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s}_{i,j}) \cdot \mathbf{w}^{(k)}(\mathbf{s}_{i,j}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{w}^{(k)}(\mathbf{s}_{i,j})$ is obtained by inputting $\{\mathbf{s}_{i,j}\}_{i,j=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B},\mathrm{G}}}$ into~\eqref{beamforming vector}. 

To compute the signal covariance matrix in~\eqref{eq:E1}, GL quadrature is also applied over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}}$. Specifically, $M_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ quadrature roots and weights $\{(\theta_m, \omega_m)\}_{m=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,G}}}$ are used to generate sampling points $\{ \hat{\mathbf{r}}_{m,n} \}_{m,n=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,G}}}$ in the local coordinate system. For the $k$-th sample, these points are mapped to global coordinates via $\mathbf{r}^{(k)}_{m,n} = \hat{\mathbf{r}}_{m,n} + \mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}$. Based on~\eqref{integral of e} and $\mathbf{r}^{(k)}_{m,n}$, applying the GL quadrature rule in~\eqref{GL of S}, the signal covariance matrix is approximated accordingly. Substituting this into~\eqref{eq:object} yields the achievable SE for the $k$-th sample. The corresponding unsupervised loss is defined as its negative value, denoted by $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,G}}^{(k)}$. The total loss over dataset  $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}$ is then computed as $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,G}} = \frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}\right|} \sum_{k=1}^{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}\right|} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,G}}^{(k)}.$



Training BeaINR with the dataset $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}$, which consists of fixed GL sampling points $\{\mathbf{s}_{i,j}\}_{i,j=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B,G}}}$ shared across all samples, may lead to overfitting to these specific coordinates and limit generalization over the aperture $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}$. To mitigate this, we introduce an auxiliary training dataset constructed using sample-wise randomized sampling based on Owen-scrambled Sobol sequences~\cite{Practical_Hash}. This approach offers two key benefits: first, Sobol sequences provide low-discrepancy sampling, enabling faster convergence in numerical integration compared to random sampling methods; second, Owen scrambling introduces controlled randomness while preserving low-discrepancy, effectively reducing overfitting and enhancing the generalization.

For each sample $k$, to generate a two-dimensional Sobol sequence of length $M_{\mathrm{B,S}}$, two independent one-dimensional sequences are constructed using distinct direction numbers. For each index $i \in \{1, \ldots, M_{\mathrm{B,S}}\}$, the index is represented as a binary-coefficient polynomial, and its coefficients are combined with the direction numbers via bitwise XOR operations to obtain the coordinate $\mathbf{s}_i^{(k)} = [s_{i,1}^{(k)}, s_{i,2}^{(k)}, 0]$. Owen scrambling applies nested, digit-wise random permutations to the series of binary coefficients before this step, resulting in a scrambled sequence with improved generalization~\cite{Practical_Hash}. The resulting sampling points $\left\{ \mathbf{s}_{i}^{(k)} \right\}_{i=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B,S}}}$, along with the corresponding user location $\mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}$, constitute the dataset
\begin{equation} \label{Dataset Ds}
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}} = \left\{ \left( \left\{ \mathbf{s}_{i}^{(k)} \right\}_{i=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B,S}}},\, \mathbf{r}_o^{(k)} \right) \right\}_{k=1}^{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}}\right|},
\end{equation}
where $\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}}\right|$ denotes the dataset size. 

The loss of the $k$-th sample in $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}}$, denoted by $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,S}}^{(k)}$, is computed following the same procedure as $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,G}}^{(k)}$, but with a different integration approximation. Specifically, the integrals in \eqref{eq:E1}, \eqref{eq:E2}, and \eqref{eq:PowerConstraint} are approximated by averaging the integrand over the Sobol sampling points and scaling by the aperture area. For instance, given the sampled points $\{\mathbf{s}_{i}^{(k)}\}_{i=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B,S}}}$, the integral of a function $f(\cdot)$ over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}$ is approximated as
\begin{align} \label{OS of S}
\int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}} f(\mathbf{s})\, d\mathbf{s}\approx \sum_{i=1}^{M_{\mathrm{B,S}}} \frac{f(\mathbf{s}^{(k)}_{i})}{M_{\mathrm{B,S}}}A_{\mathrm{B}}.
\end{align}

The total loss over dataset $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}}$ is then given by $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,S}} = \frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}}\right|} \sum_{k=1}^{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}}\right|} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,S}}^{(k)}.$

Finally, the overall loss for training BeaINR is formulated as a weighted sum of $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,G}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,S}}$, expressed as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B}} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,G}} + \lambda \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B,S}},
\label{eq:total_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda$ is a weighting factor that balances the contribution of the two loss terms.    

\section{INR of Coefficient Function}
In this section, to further reduce training complexity, we introduce CoefINR to model the coefficient function over the user aperture. Leveraging the structural property established in Proposition~1, the beamforming function is reconstructed via integration with the channel response. 

Since the user-side CAPA typically spans a smaller surface area than the BS-side CAPA, numerical integration over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}}$ requires fewer sampling points to achieve comparable spatial resolution and accuracy. This reduction in sampling points of each sample leads to a smaller dataset and thus lower training complexity. Motivated by proposition~1, we propose a dedicated INR to model the coefficient function $\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r})$ over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}}$, then $\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s})$ can be constructed via integration.



The coefficient function $\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r})$ can be  modeled by an INR as
\begin{equation} \label{INR of C}
\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r}) = \mathcal{C}_{\theta_{\mathrm{c}}}(\hat{\mathbf{r}}, \mathbf{r}_o),\quad \mathbf{r} \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{C}_{\theta_\mathrm{c}}(\cdot)$ denotes the INR-based coefficient function, termed as CoefINR, parameterized by $\theta_\mathrm{c}$, $\mathbf{r}$ and $\hat{\mathbf{r}}$ represent the same point in global and local coordinate systems, respectively. To train CoefINR, two datasets are constructed by replacing the sample points over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}$ in \eqref{Dataset D} and \eqref{Dataset Ds} with GL sampling points $\{\hat{\mathbf{r}}_{m,n}\}_{m,n=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,G}}}$ and Owen-scrambled Sobol sampling points $\left\{ \hat{\mathbf{r}}^{(k)}_{i} \right\}_{i=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,S}}}$ over $\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{U}$, respectively, where $M_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ and $M_\mathrm{U,S}$ denoting the number of GL qurature points and Sobol sampling pointes, respectively. The resulting training datasets are defined as
\begin{subequations} \label{datasets of CoefINR}
    \begin{align}
    \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}} &= \left\{ \left( \left\{ \hat{\mathbf{r}}_{m,n} \right\}_{m,n=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,G}}}, \mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}\right) \right\}_{k=1}^{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}\right|}, \label{GL datasets of CoefINR} \\
    \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}} &= \left\{ \left( \left\{ \hat{\mathbf{r}}_{i}^{(k)} \right\}_{i=1} ^{M_{\mathrm{U,S}}},\, \mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}\right) \right\}_{k=1}^{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}}\right|}, \label{SB datasets of CoefINR} 
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ and $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}}$ correspond to GL-based and Sobol-based training datasets, respectively, and $\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}\right|$ and $\left|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}}\right|$ denote their respective dataset size. 

The training of CoefINR involves two main steps: beamforming function construction and loss evaluation. In the first step, the beamforming function is constructed according to Proposition~1. Given a sample from $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ or $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}}$, CoefINR generates a specific coefficient function. For instance, for the $k$-th sample in $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}$, the coefficient function is represented~as
\begin{equation} \label{coefficient vector}
\mathbf{c}^{(k)}(\mathbf{r}) = \mathcal{C}_{\theta_c}(\hat{\mathbf{r}}, \mathbf{r}_o^{(k)}), \quad \mathbf{r}\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}},
\end{equation}
With Proposition~1, the beamforming function is expressed~as
\begin{align} \label{integral for w}
\!\!\mathbf{w}^{(k)}(\mathbf{s}) \!\approx\! \sum_{m=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,G}}} \sum_{n=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,G}}} \frac{\omega_m \omega_n A_{\mathrm{U}}}{4} \,
h(\hat{\mathbf{r}}_{m,n}, \mathbf{s}) \cdot \mathbf{c}^{(k)}(\mathbf{r}_{m,n}).
\end{align}
where $A_{\mathrm{U}} = L_{\mathrm{U},x} L_{\mathrm{U},y}$ is the user aperture area, and $\mathbf{c}^{(k)}(\mathbf{r}_{m,n})$ is obtained by substituding $\left\{ \hat{\mathbf{r}}_{m,n} \right\}_{m,n=1}^{M_{\mathrm{U,G}}}$ into \eqref{coefficient vector}. In the second step, once $\mathbf{w}^{(k)}(\mathbf{s})$ is obtained, the loss over $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ and $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}}$ is computed following the same procedure as described in Sec.~\ref{Training Method} after \eqref{beamforming vector}. The overall training loss is then obtained as a weighted sum of two loss terms, analogous to~\eqref{eq:total_loss}.

\section{Simulation Results} \label{Simulation}
In this section, we evaluate the beamforming performance of the proposed BeaINR and CoefINR by comparing them with the relevant baselines.

\subsection{Simulation Setup}
Most simulation parameters are adopted from \cite{Beamforming_Design}. The BS and user CAPAs are configured with edge length $L_{\mathrm{B},x}=L_{\mathrm{B},y}=2\,m$ and $L_{\mathrm{U},x}=L_{\mathrm{U},y}=0.5\,m$ , respectively. The position of user is expressed as $\mathbf{r}_0=[r_{x}, r_{y}, r_{z}]$, which is uniformly deployed in a region where $-5\,m<r_{x}, r_{y}<5\,m$ and $20\,m<r_{z}<30\,m$.  The carrier frequency is set to $f=2.4\,\text{GHz}$, the intrinsic impedance is $\eta=120\,\pi\Omega$. The transmit power and noise density are set to $P_T=1000\,\text{mA}^2$ and $\sigma^2=5.6\times10^{-3}\,\text{V}^2/\text{m}^2$, respectively. To ensure the maximization of the multiplexing gain, the number of data streams is set to $N=\min\{M_{\mathrm{B}}, M_{\mathrm{U}}\}$, where $M_{\mathrm{B}} = (2\left\lceil \frac{L_{{\mathrm{B}},x}}{\lambda} \right\rceil + 1)(2\left\lceil \frac{L_{{\mathrm{B}},y}}{\lambda} \right\rceil + 1)$ and $M_{\mathrm{U}} = (2\left\lceil \frac{L_{{\mathrm{U}},x}}{\lambda} \right\rceil + 1)(2\left\lceil \frac{L_{{\mathrm{U}},y}}{\lambda} \right\rceil + 1)$.

Two fully connected neural networks are employed as CoefINR and BeaINR. Both models are trained using the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.0001. We generate 500,000 samples in $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}$, $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,S}}$, $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ and  $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}}$ for training. Each sample in $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ contains $M^2_{\mathrm{U,G}}=100$ fixed GL sampling points over $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}}$, and each sample in $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,S}}$ contains $M_{\mathrm{U,S}}=400$ random Sobol sampling points. In this implementation, the numbers of sampling points are scaled to the surface area to ensure consistent sampling density across both sides. Thus, the number of the GL sequence and the Sobol sampling points over $\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{B}$ are set as $M_{\mathrm{B,G}}=\frac{L_{\mathrm{B},x}}{L_{\mathrm{U},x}}M_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ and $M_{\mathrm{B,S}}=\frac{L^2_{\mathrm{B},x}}{L^2_{\mathrm{U},x}}M_{\mathrm{U,S}}$, respectively. The weight coefficient in the loss function is set to $\lambda=0.1$.

To test the performance of the trained networks, we generate another 10,000 samples in $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{U,G}}$ and $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{B,G}}$. To more effectively assess the network's generalization capability over apertures, a higher-resolution GL grid is employed with $M_{\mathrm{U,G}}=20$ and $M_{\mathrm{B,G}}=\frac{L_{\mathrm{B},x}}{L_{\mathrm{U},x}}M_{\mathrm{U,G}}$.

\subsection{Learning Performance} 
We compare the learning performance of BeaINR and CoefINR with the numerical baselines as follows
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{WMMSE}: This method adopts the CoV-based approach from~\cite{Beamforming_Design}, where the beamforming function is optimized using an iterative WMMSE algorithm with GL quadrature and closed-form updates.

    \item \textbf{Fourier}: As proposed in~\cite{Wavenumber}, the beamforming function is expressed as a truncated Fourier series, and the optimization problem is reformulated as determining the coefficients of the selected Fourier basis functions.

    \item \textbf{SPDA}: Following the discretization strategy in~\cite{Pattern_Division}, the continuous CAPA is discretized into finite elements, reducing the problem to an SPDA formulation solvable via singular value decomposition and water-filling.
\end{itemize}  

Fig.~\ref{Learning_performance} shows the SE achieved under varying transmit power and user aperture size. Both BeaINR and CoefINR consistently outperform the Fourier-based and SPDA-based methods, owing to their ability to avoid discretization loss. They also achieve slightly higher SE than the WMMSE algorithm, with lower inference complexity. Among all methods, BeaINR yields the best SE performance.

