\section{Proof of Proposition~1} \label{appendix1}
Define the Hilbert subspace $\mathcal{H} = \text{span}\{ h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s}) : \mathbf{r} \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}} \}$. Any feasible beamforming function $\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) \in \mathbb{C}^{1 \times N}$ satisfying the power constraint~\eqref{eq:PowerConstraint} can be orthogonally decomposed as
\begin{equation} \label{decomposation}
\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) = \mathbf{w}_{\parallel}(\mathbf{s}) + \mathbf{w}_{\perp}(\mathbf{s}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{w}_{\parallel}\!\in\!\mathcal{H}$ and $\mathbf{w}_{\perp}\!\perp\!\mathcal{H}$. Substituting \eqref{decomposation} into~\eqref{eq:E2} yields
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{e}(\mathbf{r}) = \int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}} h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})\mathbf{w}_{\parallel}(\mathbf{s})d\mathbf{s},
\end{equation}
since $\int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}}\! h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})\, \mathbf{w}_{\perp}(\mathbf{s})\, d\mathbf{s}\!=\!0.$ Thus, $\mathbf{w}_{\perp}$ has no impact on the objective~\eqref{eq:object}. However, it contributes additional transmit~power:
\begin{equation}
\int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}} \| \mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) \|^2 d\mathbf{s} = \int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{B}}} \left( \| \mathbf{w}_{\parallel}(\mathbf{s}) \|^2 + \| \mathbf{w}_{\perp}(\mathbf{s}) \|^2 \right) d\mathbf{s}.
\end{equation}
Since any nonzero $\mathbf{w}_{\perp}$ contributes to power consumption without yielding any performance gains. The optimal beamforming function must lie entirely in $\mathcal{H}$, and can thus be expressed as
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{s}) = \int_{\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{U}}} h(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{s})\, \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r})\, d\mathbf{r}.
\end{equation}
which completes the proof.





