\begin{abstract}
This paper presents an operator-theoretic framework (Linear Operator Causality Analysis, \textit{LOCA}) for analyzing causality in linear dynamical systems, focusing on fluid mechanics. We demonstrate that the matrix exponential of the linearized differential equations
%$\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}t}$
fundamentally encodes the causal relationships between system modes at any future time. 
We further develop a global measure of causality that quantifies the presence and extent of global causality across all time horizons. 
%$t$, and that the matrix $\mathsfbi{M} = (\alpha\mathsfbi{I} - |\mathsfbi{A}|)^{-1}$ serves as a global measure of causality, integrating indirect causal influences over time. 
%Recognizing that $\dot{\mathsfbi{c}} = \mathsfbi{A} \mathsfbi{c}$ represents the modal formulation of the general linear operator problem $\dot{u} = L u$,
This approach provides a physics-based alternative to statistical and information-theoretic causality measures such as Granger causality and Transfer Entropy. Unlike these data-driven techniques, which infer causality from time-series data,  LOCA leverages the linearized governing equations, yielding a more rigorous and interpretable measure of causal interactions in deterministic systems. We establish that, under appropriate conditions, Granger causality and Transfer Entropy effectively measure the squared magnitude of the matrix exponential. Moreover, our causal framework reveals fundamental connections to key system properties such as controllability, observability,  and graph-theoretic transitive closure. To complement this operator-based approach, we introduce a data-driven methodology akin to Dynamic Mode Decomposition (DMD) that estimates causal connections directly from time series data by approximating the matrix exponential. LOCA also mitigates common issues in data-driven causality analyses, such as misleading inferences due to correlated variables or state truncation. To validate our methodology, we apply it to linearized Couette flow, demonstrating how our framework captures both direct and indirect causal interactions among flow structures. Through this example, we highlight the advantages of our approach, including its robustness to correlation-induced biases and its ability to identify causally significant modes. Ultimately, this framework provides a powerful tool for analyzing causality in fluid flows, offering new insights into modal interactions and the propagation of perturbations in dynamical systems.
\end{abstract}

