\section{Connections with other methods}
\label{sec:connections}
We now discuss connections between the analysis and definitions provided in \S\ref{sec:causality}. We start by discussing other notions of causality (typically applied in a data-driven manner) in \S \ref{sec:granger} before discussing connections with notions from graph theory in \S\ref{sec:graph} and observability and controllability of linear systems in \S\ref{sec:bt}.

\subsection{Connection with Granger causality and transfer entropy}
\label{sec:granger}
Here, we first discuss the connection between our operator-theoretic causality framework and the widely used statistical approach of Granger causality. We will show that under appropriate conditions, the Granger causality $F$-statistic scales with the squared magnitude of the matrix exponential element that quantifies the causal influence from one state to another.

% Consider a state $\bc$, which potentially is obtained from 
We focus on pairwise conditional Granger causality from state $c_j$ to state $c_i$ in an $m$-dimensional discrete-time system with a single time lag. Let the system state at time steps $t_n = n\,\Delta t$ be
$\bc =  \left[c_1(t_n), \dots, c_m(t_n)\right]^T$. Here, we use $\bc$ to denote the variables under consideration, given that in practice, for high-dimensional fluid flows, one must typically use a low-dimensional representation of the full system state, e.g., mapping the full state to POD coordinates via equation \ref{eq:xtoc}.

We will focus on pairwise conditional Granger analysis with a single time lag, which quantifies the causality from one variable (here taken to be $c_j$) to another ($c_i$), assuming knowledge of all other state variables. 
The analysis 
proceeds by identifying from data \emph{unrestricted} and \emph{restricted} models.  
The \emph{unrestricted} model for $c_i(t_n)$ includes past values of $c_j$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:unrestricted}
c_i(t_n) 
= \sum_{p=1}^{m} A_{ik} \, c_k(t_{n-1})
+ \epsilon^u_{i}(t_n),
\end{equation}
where $A_{ik}$ are coefficients representing the influence of $c_k$ at the previous time step on the current value of $c_i$, and $\epsilon^u_{i}(t_n)$ is the residual. The \emph{restricted} model omits $c_j$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:restricted}
c_i(t_n) 
= \sum_{\substack{k=1 \\ k \neq j}}^{m} A_{ik} \, c_k(t_{n-1})
+ \epsilon^r_{i,j}(t_n).
\end{equation}
If excluding $c_j$ significantly degrades the prediction of $c_i$, we conclude $c_j$ \emph{Granger-causes} $c_i$. Note that in the restricted model (unlike the unrestricted case), the residual $\epsilon^r_{i,j}$ will generally depend on $j$.
To evaluate the extent of causality, the approach taken by Granger causality analysis is to compare the Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) for the restricted (RSS$^r$) and unrestricted (RSS$^u$):
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{RSS}^r = \sum_{n}\bigl[\epsilon^r_{i,j}(t_n)\bigr]^2,
\quad
\mathrm{RSS}^u = \sum_{n}\bigl[\epsilon^u_{i}(t_n)\bigr]^2.
\end{equation}
through the $F$-statistic:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:F}
F 
%= \frac{(\mathrm{RSS}^r - \mathrm{RSS}^u)\,/\,1}
= \frac{\mathrm{RSS}^r - \mathrm{RSS}^u}
{\mathrm{RSS}^u \,/\,\bigl(N - (m+1)\bigr)},
\end{equation}
where the numerator reflects the single parameter ($p=1$) introduced by $c_j$, $N$ is the total number of data points, and the denominator's $(m+1)$ represents the total parameter count in the unrestricted model. If $F$ exceeds a critical threshold, we infer Granger causality from $c_j$ to $c_i$.

Now, we connect this statistical approach to our dynamical systems framework. Suppose that in continuous time, the system is locally governed by $\dot{\mathsfbi{c}}(t) = \mathsfbi{A}\,\mathsfbi{c}(t)$, so over a time step $\Delta t$,
\begin{equation}
\mathsfbi{c}(t_{n}) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\,\Delta t}\,\mathsfbi{c}(t_{n-1}).
\end{equation}
In this case, the regression coefficients $A_{ik}$ correspond to elements of the matrix exponential $[\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ik}$. Then the contribution of $c_j$ to $c_i$ over one step is given by $[\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij}\,c_j(t_{n-1})$. When $c_j$ is \emph{excluded} from the model, this omitted term appears in the restricted-model residual:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:restricted-residual-expansion}
\epsilon^r_{i,j}(t_n)
= [\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij}\,c_j(t_{n-1}) + \epsilon^u_{i}(t_n).
\end{equation}
In equation \ref{eq:restricted-residual-expansion}, we make the assumption that the retained terms in the restricted model take the same values as the equivalent terms in the unrestricted model.  
Hence,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{n} \bigl[\epsilon^r_{i,j}(t_n)\bigr]^2 
= \sum_{n} \Bigl( [\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij} \, c_j(t_{n-1}) 
      + \epsilon^u_{i}(t_n)\Bigr)^2.
\end{equation}
Expanding,
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\sum_{n} \bigl[\epsilon^r_{i,j}(t_n)\bigr]^2
&= 
([\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij})^2 \sum_{n} \bigl(c_j(t_{n-1})\bigr)^2\\
&\quad \;+\; 2\,[\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij}
    \sum_{n} c_j(t_{n-1})\,\epsilon^u_{i}(t_n)
 \;+\; \sum_{n} \bigl[\epsilon^u_{i}(t_n)\bigr]^2.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Thus, the difference between restricted and unrestricted RSS is
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathrm{RSS}^r - \mathrm{RSS}^u
&= ([\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij})^2 \sum_{n} \bigl(c_j(t_{n-1})\bigr)^2\\
&\quad \;+\; 2\,[\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij} \sum_{n} c_j(t_{n-1})\,\epsilon^u_{i}(t_n).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
If $c_j$ is uncorrelated with $\epsilon^u_{i}(t_n)$, the cross-term vanishes on average, leaving
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rss-difference}
\mathrm{RSS}^r - \mathrm{RSS}^u 
\approx 
([\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij})^2 
\sum_{n} \bigl(c_j(t_{n-1})\bigr)^2.
\end{equation}
Under the assumptions of stationarity and large $N$, $\sum_{n} (c_j(t_{n-1}))^2 \approx N \,\sigma_{c_j}^2$. We also expect $\sum_{n} [\epsilon^u_{i}(t_n)]^2 \approx N\,\sigma_{\epsilon,i}^2$, where (as in equation \ref{eq:F1}) $\sigma_{\epsilon,i}$ is the variance of the error in the equation for the $i$-th state. Thus,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:matrixexponential}
\begin{split}
F 
&\approx 
\frac{([\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij})^2\, N\, \sigma_{c_j}^2 / 1}
{N\, \sigma_{\epsilon}^2 / (N-(m+1))}\\
&\;\;\approx\;\;
\frac{([\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij})^2\, \sigma_{c_j}^2(N-(m+1))}{\sigma_{\epsilon}^2},
\end{split}
\end{equation}
showing that the Granger causality $F$-statistic scales with $\bigl([\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij}\bigr)^2$. Since the primary focus of this work is to perform causality analysis directly using the linearized dynamics rather than data, we can exclude the term proportional to the number of timesteps of data $(N-(m+1))$ to obtain an expression equivalent to equation \ref{eq:F1}. It is this quantity that will be considered henceforth when quantifying causality. 

We can thus conclude that subject to the assumptions of this analysis (most notably that the restricted and unrestricted models share the same coefficients except for those involving the excluded variable), our notion of causality defined in equation  \ref{eq:F1} agrees with Granger analysis.
Since transfer entropy and Granger causality are equivalent for linear Gaussian processes~\citep{Barnett2012TransferEA}, an extension of this result is also that transfer entropy, under appropriate restrictions, is also equivalent, with the causality dependent on $\bigl([\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]_{ij}\bigr)^2$. Note that in transfer entropy, causality is typically quantified using a log-likelihood quantity rather than an $F$-statistic, where the equivalent to equation \ref{eq:F1} can be shown to be 
\begin{equation}
    F^{(L)}_{j\to i} = \log \left(\frac{1+[\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}\Delta t}]^2_{ij} \sigma_{c_j}^2}{\sigma_{\epsilon}^2} \right).
\end{equation}
In some works, this quantity is also used within the Granger framework \citep{barnett2014mvgc,barnett2015granger}.

