\section{Causal inference from data}\label{sec:causal-inference}

Since causality is embedded in the system matrix $\mathsfbi{A}$, it is interesting to see if the elements of this matrix can be ascertained from data. This can be framed as an optimization problem, and there are likely multiple ways of achieving this objective, each with its advantages and disadvantages. Here, we propose one method of doing so, which is based on the Dynamic Mode Decomposition \citep{Schmid2008DynamicMD} technique. Dynamic Mode Decomposition (DMD) is a data-driven technique used to approximate the underlying dynamics of a complex system from time-series data. In the context of a linear dynamical system, DMD allows us to estimate the system matrix, which can then be used to infer causal relationships between the components. To estimate the system matrix $\mathsfbi{A}$, we generate data by simulating the system for multiple randomly chosen initial conditions $\mathsfbi{c}_0 \in \mathbb{C}^n$, where the initial conditions are drawn from a complex-valued distribution. The system is then evolved forward, yielding a time series for each component of $\mathsfbi{c}(t)$. The numerical integration provides a discrete set of time points, $t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_m$, at which the state vector is recorded, forming a dataset.

Let $\mathsfbi{X} \in \mathbb{C}^{n \times m}$ denote the data matrix, where each column of $\mathsfbi{X}$ represents the state vector at a specific time step. We define two data matrices,
\begin{equation}
    \mathsfbi{X}_1 = [\mathsfbi{c}(t_1), \mathsfbi{c}(t_2), \ldots, \mathsfbi{c}(t_{m-1})],
    \quad
    \mathsfbi{X}_2 = [\mathsfbi{c}(t_2), \mathsfbi{c}(t_3), \ldots, \mathsfbi{c}(t_m)],
\end{equation}
which captures the system's state at consecutive time steps. The goal of DMD here is to find an approximate linear map $\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}}$ such that
\begin{equation}
    \mathsfbi{X}_2 \approx \mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}} \mathsfbi{X}_1.
\end{equation}

To solve for $\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}}$, we use the least-squares solution given by
\begin{equation}
    \mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}} = \mathsfbi{X}_2 \mathsfbi{X}_1^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
where $\mathsfbi{X}_1^{\dagger}$ denotes the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse of $\mathsfbi{X}_1$. This approach yields the matrix $\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}}$ that best approximates the linear mapping from $\mathsfbi{X}_1$ to $\mathsfbi{X}_2$ in a least-squares sense. This estimated matrix $\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}}$ gives us a relation between $\mathsfbi{c}(t_{m+1})$ and $\mathsfbi{c}(t_{m})$.
\begin{comment}
    Therefore, $\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{est}}=(\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}}-\mathsfbi{I})/\Delta t$ is roughly equivalent to the estimate of $\mathsfbi{A}$.
\end{comment} 
Accordingly, \(\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{est}} = (\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{DMD}} - \mathsfbi{I})/\Delta t\) provides a first-order estimate of the continuous-time system matrix $\mathsfbi{A}$ associated with the underlying dynamics.
We can use the elements of $\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{est}}$ to now perform causality analysis by computing $\mathsfbi{M}=(\mathsfbi{I}-|\mathsfbi{A}_{\mathrm{est}}|)^{-1}$.

