0.5 & 0 & -3
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align}




These results for two different stable $\mathsfbi{A}$ matrices are presented in Fig. (\ref{fig:causality_matrices}). Fig. (\ref{fig:causality_matrices}a) corresponds to an upper triangular $\mathsfbi{A}^{(1)}$ matrix with eigenvalues $-1,-2,-3$ (see Eq. \ref{eq:exampleA}). The DMD estimate results in a matrix with eigenvalues $-1,-2,-3.002$, giving a normed error of $0.0018$ in the estimate of the eigenvalues. The $\mathsfbi{M}$ matrix corresponding to this case is also upper triangular, showing no causal flow from $1\rightarrow 2,1\rightarrow 3,2\rightarrow 3$. Fig. (\ref{fig:causality_matrices}b) corresponds to an upper triangular $\mathsfbi{A}^{(2)}$ matrix with a nonzero term in the bottom left corner. The normed error in eigenvalue estimate is small ($0.0017$) and it is interesting to note that even though $A^{(2)}_{21}=A^{(2)}_{32}=0$, $M^{(2)}_{21},M^{(2)}_{32}\neq0$ indicating indirect channels of global causality. Although not shown here, we have tested our framework for larger systems, where it emerges as a highly data-efficient technique for gauging causality.

