Thus far, this example has focused on quantitative causality assessments over a specified time horizon. We now consider the global causality metric $\mM$ defined in equation \ref{eq:M}. In figure \ref{fig:chanM}, we compute this quantity in the original ($v,\eta$) coordinates of the system. The top right block is zero (to numerical precision), indicating no causal connection from $\eta$ to $v$. This is expected since the lower block triangular structure of $\mL$ in equation \ref{eq:chan} is retained in the discretized system (equation \ref{eq:chanDisc}) for all time horizons. Conversely, $\mM$ identifies causal connections between all other state variables.


