\section{Conclusions}

In this paper, we have developed an operator-theoretic framework for analyzing causality in linear dynamical systems, particularly those arising in fluid mechanics. Our approach leverages the fundamental insight that for systems in the canonical form $\dot{\mathsfbi{c}}=\mathsfbi{A}\mathsfbi{c}$, the matrix exponential $\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}t}$ encodes causal connections between modes at any future time $t$. In contrast, the matrix $\mathsfbi{M}=(\alpha\mathsfbi{I}-|\mathsfbi{A}|)^{-1}$ provides a measure of global causality integrated over all time. This framework offers a physics-based alternative to statistical and information-theoretic approaches, such as Granger causality and Transfer Entropy, which do not inherently utilize the governing equations available in many deterministic systems.

We have established that causality in linear systems can be characterized at three distinct levels: (1) immediate causality, determined by non-zero off-diagonal elements of $\mathsfbi{A}$; (2) delayed causality, captured by the matrix exponential $\mathrm{e}^{\mathsfbi{A}t}$ at various time horizons; and (3) global causality, represented by the matrix $\mathsfbi{M}$. Our analysis reveals that even when immediate causality is absent (i.e., when $A_{ij}=0$), delayed or global causality can emerge through indirect pathways involving intermediate modes, a phenomenon elegantly captured by the power series expansion of the matrix exponential.

The operator-theoretic causality framework demonstrates deep connections with several fundamental concepts in dynamical systems theory. We have shown that the absence of global causality between specific modes ($M_{ij}=0$) directly implies limitations in both controllability and observability when control inputs or measurements are restricted to those modes. This relationship extends to the controllability and observability Gramians, offering potential guidance for optimal sensor and actuator placement in practical applications.

We have demonstrated that under appropriate conditions, both Granger causality and Transfer Entropy effectively measure the squared magnitude of the matrix exponential. However, our analysis of linearized Couette flow reveals important distinctions between these statistical approaches and our operator-based method, particularly in systems with correlated variables. When applied to uncorrelated POD modes, all methods converge to similar results, confirming our established theoretical relationship. Notably, our analysis %of linearized channel flow 
 reveals that the most causally significant interactions can be highly sensitive to the time horizon considered and may involve lower-energy modes that significantly impact the dynamics of higher-energy structures. This finding underscores the importance of considering the full range of modes when analyzing causal mechanisms, as truncated representations may omit crucial causal pathways. For practical applications where the system matrix is unknown, we have developed a data-driven approach based on Dynamic Mode Decomposition to estimate causal connections directly from time series data. This method effectively bridges the gap between purely theoretical analysis and purely data-driven approaches, allowing for robust causal inference in complex systems with minimal data requirements.

Overall, our operator-theoretic framework for causality provides a powerful analytical tool for understanding the complex modal interactions in fluid flows and other linear dynamical systems. By leveraging the underlying physics rather than relying solely on statistical correlations, this approach offers deeper insight into the causal mechanisms driving system behavior, with significant implications for predicting, controlling, and designing fluid systems.


