The following result follows from Corollary \ref{cor881} and \cite[Theorem 1.5]{TZ04} (quoted as Lemma \ref{u5n} below).

\begin{theor}\label{u55}
Let $G$ be a finite simple group of Lie type, and let $u\in G$ be a unipotent element. 

$(1)$ If $q$ is odd then $e(u)\leq 3$ unless $G=E_8(q)$ and $5|q$, in which case $e(u)\leq 6.$ 

$(2)$ If $q$ is even and $G \not \in \{\Sz(q)={}^2B_2(q),F_4(q), E_7(q),E_8(q)\}$ then $e(u)=2$.

$(3)$ If $q$ is even and $G\in\{F_4(q)$, $E_7(q)\}$ then $e(u) \leq 4$. If $G=\Sz(q)$ then $e(u)\leq 3$.

$(4)$ If $G=E_8(q)$, $q$ even then $e(u) \leq 8$.
\end{theor}

Corollary \ref{cor881} is stated for an arbitrary finite group $G$.
Assuming that  $g\in G$ is of odd prime power order and the generators of the cyclic group $\langle g\rangle$ lie in at most two conjugacy classes of $G$, it claims that   $e(g)\leq3$.  In turn,  \cite[Theorem 1.5]{TZ04} claims that this assumption holds (with a few exceptions) 
for the unipotent elements of finite groups of Lie type.

This motivated our interest to elements of the above kind. We focus on such elements $g\in G$ with $e(g)>2$. Specifically, we define:

\begin{defi} \label{defsemirational}
Let $G$ be a finite group and $g\in G$. We say that $g$ is semirational if every generator of $\langle g \rangle$ is conjugate to $g$ or $g^{-1}$.
\end{defi}

