{\it Notation} For integers $m,n$ we write for $(m,n)$ for the greatest common divisor of $m,n$. $\diag(A_1,\ldots,A_k)$ a block-diagonal matrix with diagonal blocks $A_1,\ldots,A_k$.

 If $F$ is a field $F^\times$ means the multiplicative group of $F$. $\mathbb{F}_q$ 
a finite field of $q$ elements.  $\overline{\mathbb{F}}_q$ the algebraic closure of  $\mathbb{F}_q$. 

If $G$ is a group and $g\in G$ then $\langle g \rangle$ denote the cyclic group generated by $g$. By $\Phi(g)$ we denote the set of generators of $\langle g \rangle$. For a prime $p$
we denote by $Z(G), G'$ and $O_p(G) $ the center, the derived subgroup and the maximal normal $p$-subgroup of $G$.

If $G$ is a group of Lie type in defining characteristic $p$ then
$p$-elements are called unipotent and $p'$-elements are called semisimple. 

Our notation for simple groups of Lie type are standard, for sporadic groups we follow \cite{at}.

If $x,y$ are elements of a group $G$ then $x^y$ means $x^{-1}yx$ and we write $x \sim y$ if   they are conjugate in $G$. %, i.e. there exists $g \in G$ such that $g^{-1}xg = y$.

If $g \in G$, we denote by $|g|$ the (usual) order of $g$ and by $e(g)=e_G(g)$ generalized order, that is, the smallest positive integer $k$ such that $1 \in C^k$, where $C$ is the conjugacy class of $g$ in $G$. We call $k$ the exponent of $C$.

