\subsection{Some known facts about finite groups of Lie type}

Recall that a group of Lie type $G$ is defined as $C_{\mathbf{G}}(\si)$, where   $\mathbf{G}$ is a reductive connected algebraic group and $\sigma:\mathbf{G}\rightarrow\mathbf{G}$
is an algebraic group homomorphism such that  $G:=C_{\mathbf{G}}(\sigma)$ is finite \cite[Theorem 21.5]{MTe}.
A standard notation for $C_{\mathbf{G}}(\sigma)$ is $\mathbf{G}^\sigma$. Then $\si$
is called a Frobenius (or Steinberg) endomorphism of $\mathbf{G}$.  

All simple groups of Lie type of the form $G'/Z(G')$ for $\mathbf{G} $ a simple algebraic group with some exceptions 
(given in \cite[Theorem 24.17]{MTe}). Let $G$ be a group of Lie type (often referred as a finite reductive group) and let $p$ be the defining characteristic of $G$.  
We define a regular semisimple element $s\in G$ as one with $C_G(s)$ a $p'$-group;   the latter is equivalent to saying that  $C_{\mathbf{G}}(s)$ has no non-trivial unipotent  element
(see for instance \cite[\S 2.3]{Hm} and \cite[Proposition  14.23]{DM}).  In particular, if
$g\in G=\mathbf{G}^\sigma$ is a regular semisimple element then $g$ is regular semisimple
in $\mathbf{G}$ and in $\mathbf{G}^{\sigma^k}$ for $k>2$.

We   often use the following fact: if $V$ is a finite dimensional $F$-vector space, where $F$ is a finite field, then in the group $\GL(V)$ any semisimple irreducible element is regular. Indeed, if $s$ is a semisimple irreducible element and $u$ is a unipotent element commuting with $s$, then $u-1$ induces a $F\langle s \rangle$-morphism $V \to V$ that is not an isomorphism, so $u-1=0$ by Schur's lemma, in other words $u=1$.



