%\begin{proof} Let $g=u_1u_2\in C $. Set $x=u_2gu_2^{-1}=u_2u_1$, and $gx=u_1u_2^2u_1\in C^2$.This is conjugate to $u_1^2u_2^2\in C^2$. Therefore, $u_1^{2^k}u_2^{2^k}\in C^{2^k}$. Note that the exponent of a Sylow 2-subgroup of $G$ equals $2^4=16$, see for instance \cite[Table 5, p. 4136]{Lw}.Therefore, $u_i^{16}=1$ for $i=1,2$. \end{proof}


\begin{theor} \label{ue67} If $G$ is of type $E_7(q)$ or  $E_8(q)$ and $q$ is even then $e(G)\leq 4$, $e(G)\leq 8$ respectively. If $G=E_8(q)$ and $5$ divides $q$, then $e(G) \leq 6$. \end{theor}

\begin{proof}
There is a maximal parabolic subgroup $P$ of 
$G$ such that, writing $U=O_p(P)$ as the unipotent radical of $P$, where $p$ is the prime number dividing $q$, we have $(P/U)' \cong E_6(q)$ or $\Omega^+_{14}(\mathbb{F}_q)$, respectively, for $E_7(q)$, $E_8(q)$. Indeed, by general theory of parabolic subgroups of simple algebraic groups  \cite[\S 12.1]{MTe}, the parabolic subgroups of $G$ are determined by a subsets of the nodes at the Dynkin diagram $D$ of $G$,
and the semisimple component of $P/U$ corresponds to the subdiagram obtained by removing 
from $D$ the nodes determining $P$. In case of $E_7,E_8$ use the node 7, 1, respectively. Then the subdiagram obtained is of type $E_6, D_7$, respectively. As $q$ is even, the universal group of 
type $D_7(q)$ has trivial center, so this is isomorphic to $\Omega^+_{14}(\mathbb{F}_q)$.  As $q$ is even, by \cite[Theorem 1.8(ii,iii)]{TZ04}, every unipotent element of $(P/U)'$ is real. 

%Note that $(P/U)'$ %


The index of $(P/U)'$ in $P/U$ is odd. For this observe that $P=LU$, a semidirect product, where $L\cong P/U$ is a Levi subgroup of $P$ (see \cite[Proposition 12.6.3]{MTe}), and $P=LU$.  
Let $\mathbf{G}$ be the simple algebraic group such that $G=\mathbf{G}^F$ for a Frobenius endomorphism $F$ of $\mathbf{G}$. By \cite[Proposition  26.1]{MTe},
$P=\mathbf{P}^F$ and $L= \mathbf{L}^F$ for a suitable parabolic subgroup $\mathbf{P}$
of $\mathbf{G}$ and a Levi subgroup $\mathbf{L}$ of $\mathbf{P}$.  
By \cite[Proposition  12.6]{MTe}, $\mathbf{L}$ is a connected reductive group.  Note that a simple algebraic group of type $E_8$
 is   of simply connected type \cite[Table 9.2]{MTe}. As  $q$ even,  a simple algebraic group of type  $E_7$ is isomorphic to that of simply connected type.  So we can assume this of simply connected type too.   Then   $L'$  is   of simply connected type \cite[Proposition  12.14]{MTe}.  
Therefore, $(L')^F$ is generated by the unipotent elements of $L^F$  \cite[Theorem 24.15]{MTe}, and hence quasisimple by
\cite[Cor 24.17]{MTe}, in fact simple as $Z((L')^F)=1$. In addition, $|L^F/(L')^F|$ is odd. 

%In addition, $L$ is a split BN-pair group (\cite[Prop 2.6.3]{C}), and if $L_1$ is the subgroup of $L$ generated by the unipotent elements of $L$ then $L/L_1$ is an abelian group of odd order, see the expression for $L$ written just after the statement of Proposition 2.6.3 in \cite{C}. So $L'\subseteq L_1$. As $L_1$ is quasisimple \textcolor{red}{(Why is $L_1$ quasisimple?)}, we have $L'=L_1$ so $|L/L'|$ is odd.

Let $g$ be a unipotent element in $P$. Then $gU \in (P/U)'$ by the above paragraph, since the order of $gU$ in  $P/U$ is a power of $2$. Since the unipotent elements of $(P/U)'$ are real, there exist $u_1,u_2\in L'U$ conjugate to $g$ in $L'U$ such that $u_1u_2\in U$. 
%there are 3 conjugate of $y$ in $P/U$ whose product $x$ lie in $U$. (If $p=2$, $y$ is real.) 
The structure of $U$ is known, in particular, the consecutive factors of the lower central series of $U$ are of exponent 2 \cite[Proposition  17.3]{MTe}.  
%Note that the  maximal parabolic subgroup of $G$ are labeled by the nodes of the Dynkin diagram of $G,$ and one can choose node 6,1, resp.,  for choosing $P. $
If  $G=E_7(q)$, then $U$ is abelian of exponent 2 \cite[Table 5]{Ko}, and hence $e(g)\leq 4$. If  $G=E_8(q)$, then $U$ is nilpotent of class 2 (see \cite[Table 5]{Ko}), and hence $U$ is of exponent at most $4$ (in fact, equal to $4$). Since $P$ contains a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$, we conclude that $e(g)\leq 8$ if $g\in G$ is unipotent.

Let $g=su=us$, with $s$ semisimple and $u$ unipotent. If $s=1$ then $g$ is unipotent,
so we are done. Suppose that $s \neq 1$. If $G=E_7(q)$, then every semisimple component of $C_G(s)$ is either classical or of type $E_6$ and $u$ is real in $C_G(s)$, see the proof of Theorem 10.13 in \cite{TZ04}; as $s$ is real in $G$, we conclude that $e(g)\leq 4$. Assume now that $G=E_8(q)$ with $q$ even. Then every semisimple component of $C_G(s)$ is either classical or of type $E_6,E_7$. This follows from Table 5.1 in \cite{LSS} as $C_G(s)$  contains a maximal torus of $G$. Since $u\in C_G(s)$, by the above the generalized exponent of $u$ in  $ C_G(s)$
 is at most 4, and $s$ is real. Hence $e(g)\leq 8$. 

 Let $G=E_8(q)$ with $5$ dividing $q$, and let $g \in G$. Let $g=su$, where $s,u$ are powers of $g$, $s$ is semisimple and $|u|$ is a $5$-power. 

Suppose first that  $s=1$ so $g=u$ is unipotent. Then $u^{25}=1$ by \cite{Lw}. 
By \cite[the proof of Lemma 10.12]{TZ04}, $u$ is $G$-conjugate to $u^l$ if $l\equiv 1\pmod 5$. In particular, the elements  $u,u^6$ are conjugate. As $u \cdot (u^6)^4 = u^{25} = 1$, we have $e(u)\leq 5$.

If $u=1$ then $g=s$ is real by \cite[Proposition 3.11(ii)]{TZ05}, so $e(g)=2$. So we assume $s\neq 1$, $u\neq 1$, in particular, $s$ is not regular. 

Let $\mathbf{G}$ be the simple algebraic group of type $ E_8$, and let
$\sigma$ be the Frobenius endomorphism of $\mathbf{G}$ such that $G=C_{\mathbf{G}}(\sigma)$. Note that this group is usually denoted by $\mathbf{G}^\sigma$ in the literature, and we use this notation below whenever it is convenient. Obviously, we have $C_G(s)=C_{\mathbf{G}}(s)^\sigma$. We can apply \cite[Theorem 1.5]{TZ04} to $C_G(s)$ as soon as we observe that $p=5$ is almost good for $C_G(s)$, equivalently, $C_{\mathbf{G}}(s)^\sigma$
has no composition factor of type $E_8$ and $C_G(s)$ has no composition factor that is a Suzuki group, see  \cite[p. 328]{TZ04}. The former is true by Lemma \ref{e88}. 

Observe that the non-abelian composition factors of $C_G(s)$ are simple groups of Lie type in defining characteristic $5$, none of which is a Suzuki group ${}^2B_2(2^{2k+1})$, 
the abelian composition factors are $5'$-groups. This facts follow for instance
from  \cite[Lemma 2.2]{TZ04}. (In the degenerate case with 
  $k=0$ the group  ${}^2B_2(2^{2})$ has order 20 \cite[Table 24.1, p. 208]{MTe}, hence a normal subgroup of order $5$.) Now, by  
\cite[Theorem 1.5]{TZ04}, the generators of $\langle u \rangle$ lie in at most two conjugacy classes
of $C_G(s)$. So $e_{C}(u)\leq 3$ by Corollary \ref{cor881}.
Therefore,  the product of at most $3$ conjugates of $u$ in $C_u$ lies in $\langle s\rangle$. As $s$ is real \cite[Proposition  3.1(ii)]{TZ05}, it follows that $e(g)\leq 6$.    
\end{proof}

