\section{Introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
The knowledge economy, characterized by the production and management of expertise as a key economic resource, has relied on organizational hierarchies to efficiently allocate and utilize specialized knowledge \citep{powell2004knowledge}. This has often taken the form of a ``knowledge-based hierarchy," where knowledge of solutions to common or simpler problems resides at the lower level, while knowledge of more complex problems resides in higher layers of the hierarchy. When lower-level workers encounter problems beyond their capacity, these are escalated to specialists or managers at higher levels \citep{garicano2000hierarchies}. However, the advent of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) is fundamentally challenging this established order by enabling machines to perform complex knowledge-based tasks through natural language interactions, potentially transforming how organizations structure and deploy workforce. 

GenAI's impact on organizational structure is unprecedented in its scope and nature. Unlike previous technological advances that primarily automated routine tasks or augmented specific technical capabilities, GenAI can process and generate complex knowledge outputs across diverse domains---from legal analysis and financial modeling to software development and strategic planning. For instance, financial institutions report junior analysts using GenAI to perform sophisticated market analyses traditionally reserved for senior staff, while law firms leverage GenAI tools like Harvey to enable paralegals to draft complex legal documents previously requiring experienced attorneys. Goldman Sachs estimates that GenAI could impact up to 300 million jobs globally, with 7\% potentially being substituted and 63\% being complemented by the technology, suggesting a fundamental reshaping of organizational knowledge hierarchies \citep{hatzius2023potentially}. Additionally, recent studies demonstrate that GenAI tools can substantially boost productivity in various business areas. On average, GenAI increased users' output by 66\% in performing realistic tasks. In customer support, agents managed 13.8\% more inquiries per hour \citep{brynjolfsson2023generative}; business professionals produced 59\% more documents per hour \citep{NoyShakked2023Eeot}; and programmers completed 126\% more projects weekly \citep{peng2023impact}.

However, GenAI introduces a critical challenge: hallucinations---plausible but incorrect outputs generated without warning \citep{bubeck2023sparks}. Hallucinations, which can include erroneous references, content, and statements, often coexist with correct information and are presented in a persuasive and confident manner, posing challenges for their identification without thorough scrutiny and diligent fact-checking. The real-world consequences of GenAI hallucinations have already emerged across various professional sectors. In a notable legal incident, attorneys faced a \$5,000 penalty for submitting court documents containing fictional case citations generated by GenAI, which they had mistakenly verified as legitimate \citep{Shin2023}. The healthcare sector faces even more critical concerns, as GenAI hallucinations could directly impact patient safety, leading medical professionals to worry about potential malpractice litigation \citep{Mello2023}. These pervasive hallucinations create a critical reliability gap in GenAI-assisted workflows, forcing organizations to carefully balance GenAI capabilities against the risks of automated errors. In response, organizations are increasingly implementing human-in-the-loop validation in their workflow, often positioning managers as gatekeepers responsible for overseeing GenAI-assisted outputs \citep{ringman_hallucinations_2023}. 

As organizations navigate this transformation, three critical questions emerge about the relationship between GenAI and organizational structure:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{How do advances in GenAI's capabilities and reliability affect workforce composition and hierarchical structures?} This question addresses the unique nature of GenAI's impact compared to traditional technologies. Previous studies on technological progress, such as those focused on earlier waves of information technology, have generally shown that advanced technologies complement higher-skilled labor, thereby increasing demand for higher-skilled workers \citep[e.g.,][]{bartel2007does}. GenAI, by contrast, presents a novel challenge to this trend by potentially reducing the reliance on managerial support, allowing lower-skilled workers to perform complex tasks beyond their expertise with GenAI assistance \citep{autor2024applying}. The critical question of the new era of GenAI is whether this technology will primarily accelerate the existing trend associated with AI adoption: flattening of organizational hierarchy and upskilling of workforce composition \citep{babina2023firm}.\footnote{In economics, \textit{deskilling} refers to the replacement of skilled labor within an industry or economy with technologies that enable tasks to be performed by semi-skilled or unskilled workers. In contrast, \textit{upskilling} involves raising workforce skill levels, and replacing lower-skilled labor with higher-skilled workers in response to evolving job requirements \citep{rafner2022deskilling}.} 

    \item \textit{As organizations implement human-in-the-loop validation to mitigate hallucination risks, how does this oversight mechanism influence GenAI adoption and managerial roles?} 
    This question addresses a critical gap in existing theories of organizational structure, which have not considered how human-in-the-loop validation requirements might counteract technology's traditional role in expanding the managerial \textit{span of control} (the ratio of low-level workers to high-level managers) \citep{bloom2014distinct}. The need for human oversight creates a novel tension in organizational design: while GenAI can reduce workers' reliance on managerial assistance for problem-solving, its hallucination risks may actually increase the need for managerial oversight. As a result, whether GenAI adoption would expand or narrow the span of control remains a puzzle. Resolving this tension is crucial for updating theories of knowledge-based hierarchies to account for GenAI-assisted workflows. 

    \item \textit{How do GenAI-driven productivity improvements distinctly reshape organizational structures compared to previous technological advances?}  The emergence of GenAI has demonstrated remarkable capabilities in supporting complex professional work, from medical diagnosis to legal document preparation and software development. This unprecedented ability to assist in knowledge-intensive domains has sparked widespread predictions of significant productivity enhancements across industries \citep{baily2023machines}. While productivity improvements are not unique to GenAI, the impact of GenAI-driven productivity gains could manifest differently due to complex trade-offs between efficiency gains, skill requirements, and oversight needs---particularly when considering hallucination risks. Understanding these dynamics is crucial for organizations as they adapt their structures, roles, and workflows to effectively leverage GenAI. Moreover, examining how productivity gains from GenAI translate into structural changes could reveal important implications for both managerial roles and worker welfare that differ from patterns observed with previous technologies.
\end{enumerate}

To address these questions, we develop a theoretical framework examining the interaction between GenAI technologies and organizational structure. Grounded in organization science and economics theories, we propose a model of knowledge-based hierarchy with two distinct tiers: lower-level workers and higher-level managers. In this knowledge economy, managers assist workers with problems beyond their expertise, though this support involves communication costs. GenAI introduces a new dynamic by enabling workers to address challenges exceeding their knowledge boundaries, potentially reducing their dependence on managerial guidance. However, this technological assistance comes with the risk of hallucinations. While human-in-the-loop validation can effectively mitigate these hallucinations, it introduces additional costs in both validation processes and communication requirements. 

\textbf{Main Results:} Our analysis reveals that firms should adopt GenAI only when hallucination rates fall below a critical threshold. As GenAI advances through improved capabilities or reduced hallucination rates, organizations strategically lower worker knowledge requirements for two reasons: enhanced capabilities enable workers to handle more complex problems beyond their expertise, while reduced hallucination rates lower the risks of GenAI-assisted problem solving. This combination allows firms to reduce compensation costs by hiring less skilled workers while maintaining operational effectiveness through GenAI augmentation, leading to a ``\textit{deskilling effect}" in the workforce. Regarding organizational structure, one might expect that as GenAI capabilities increase or hallucination rates decrease, workers would rely more heavily on GenAI for complex tasks, thereby reducing the need for managerial support and broadening the span of control. However, our study reveals an interesting result: enhanced GenAI capabilities or reduced hallucination rates may actually narrow the span of control, due to the deskilling effect and increased demand for managerial assistance. These findings challenge common assumptions about AI's role in flattening organizational hierarchies.

To address hallucination risks, organizations increasingly implement human-in-the-loop validation, where managers review GenAI-assisted outputs before implementation. This additional layer of manager validation has implications for both organizational hierarchy and GenAI adoption conditions. One might expect human-in-the-loop validation to narrow the span of control, as managers must divide their time between validating GenAI-assisted outputs and assisting workers, seemingly limiting their supervisory capacity. However, our analysis uncovers an unexpected result: adding manager validation can expand the span of control, thereby reducing the number of managers needed. This occurs because high validation costs force firms to employ workers with higher knowledge levels to limit GenAI usage. The higher knowledge level of workers, in turn, lightens managers' workloads. Furthermore, human-in-the-loop validation can influence GenAI adoption differently depending on the interaction between hallucination rates and validation costs. In cases with low hallucination rates but high validation costs, validation may deter GenAI adoption. Conversely, when hallucination rates are high and validation costs are low, firms can cost-effectively mitigate significant hallucination losses through validation, thus making GenAI adoption more feasible.

Lastly, we examine a third essential dimension---productivity improvement---which, although not unique to GenAI, unfolds in distinct ways within post-GenAI organizations. As productivity rises, organizations shift toward employing fewer but more knowledgeable workers to minimize hallucination risks, leading to an ``\textit{upskilling effect}" in the workforce. These higher-skilled workers require less oversight, expanding managerial spans of control until, at a critical productivity threshold, organizations transform into single-layer structures of highly skilled autonomous workers. This pattern contrasts sharply with traditional technology-driven productivity gains, which typically preserve existing organizational structures. While GenAI productivity improvements consistently boost firm profits, their welfare effects vary across organizational levels. Managers face declining roles as spans of control expand, while workers experience competing effects: reduced total employment lowers aggregate income, but higher expertise requirements increase individual wages. Our analysis reveals that workers benefit from these productivity gains only within a specific intermediate range of productivity improvements.

\textbf{Our Contributions:} This paper advances our understanding of technological disruption in organizational structures in several important ways. First, our study makes a novel contribution by explicitly modeling the tension between GenAI's enhancement of worker capabilities and its hallucination risks---a critical feature absent in previous technologies. By incorporating human-in-the-loop validation into our theoretical framework, we extend the literature on organizational adaptation to technological change by identifying the specific structural modifications required to effectively integrate GenAI while maintaining operational reliability. Second, our research directly addresses calls in the literature for theoretical frameworks to understand how GenAI technologies reshape organizational structures and the future of work \citep{babina2023firm, eloundou2024gpts}. We show how the simultaneous effects of knowledge democratization and hallucination risks reshape both horizontal and vertical dimensions of organizational hierarchy---affecting everything from worker skill requirements and wage disparities to managerial roles and spans of control. These findings contribute to the emerging literature on AI-driven organizational transformation \citep{agrawal2023turing, liu2024does, babina2023firm}. Third, by developing a formal model that captures GenAI's most distinctive characteristics and their organizational implications, we provide a foundation for understanding how firms can optimally restructure their operations to harness the benefits of GenAI while effectively managing its unique challenges. As organizations increasingly adopt GenAI---with adoption rates expected to reach 80\% by 2026, up from less than 5\% in 2023\footnote{\href{https://www.weforum.org/stories/2024/04/ceos-cfos-take-note-3-key-ways-to-adopt-genai-successfully/}{https://www.weforum.org/stories/2024/04/ceos-cfos-take-note-3-key-ways-to-adopt-genai-successfully/}}---understanding these structural implications becomes crucial for maintaining competitive advantage while ensuring operational reliability. 

\par The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{section-literature} compares and contrasts our paper with the related literature. Section \ref{section-model} outlines the model setup for both pre-GenAI and post-GenAI organizations. Section \ref{section-analysis} explores how firms integrate GenAI into their operations and how advancements in GenAI technology reshape knowledge hierarchies and organizational structures. Section \ref{section-validation} examines the role of human-in-the-loop validation in organizational hierarchies and GenAI adoption. Section \ref{section-productivity} investigates the impact of GenAI-driven productivity improvements. We conclude in Section \ref{section-conclusion}. All proofs and supplemental materials are presented in the appendices. 
