\section{Literature Review}\label{section-literature}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
The main contribution of this paper is the development of a new model of human-GenAI collaboration in the knowledge economy to analyze how GenAI adoption and improvements in its unique features impact labor allocation and skill hierarchies. In doing so, we contribute to two emerging research streams: organizational theory, particularly knowledge-based hierarchies, and the impacts of AI on labor and workforce.

There is a large body of literature on organizational theory that emerged from \cite{garicano2000hierarchies}. In the model, the role of hierarchy is to facilitate the acquisition of knowledge by increasing its utilization rate. The framework of \cite{garicano2000hierarchies} and the knowledge-based hierarchies that it rationalized have been used to address many economic questions \citep{garicano2004inequality, fuchs2015optimal}. Several recent papers generalize the setting of \cite{garicano2000hierarchies} by allowing: product position \citep{wu2015organizational}; delay cost \citep{chen2019comparative}; heterogeneity in the communication costs \citep{gumpert2018organization}; heterogeneity in decision types \citep{bloom2014distinct}. \cite{garicano2015knowledge} provide a survey of this literature on knowledge-based hierarchies. Despite the burgeoning interest in the economic study of organizational design, organizational economists have not yet paid much explicit attention to the workforce implications of GenAI, which is profoundly different from previous waves of technology innovations. We extend this research by integrating GenAI's distinctive features into a formal analytical model, offering insights into its impact on organizational design and workforce dynamics.

Though GenAI is new, earlier AI's impact on labor and workforce has been extensively studied \citep{fugener2021will, babina2023firm, wang2024friend, babina2024artificial, ide2024artificial}. One research stream enhances theoretical frameworks to understand AI-labor relations. \cite{acemoglu2019automation} argue that AI and robotics significantly displace human labor but also highlight mitigating factors. \cite{agrawal2019artificial} categorize jobs into prediction and decision tasks, suggesting AI's ambiguous impact. Another research strand provides empirical evidence of AI's dual effects on the labor market. \cite{lysyakov2023threatened} show that lower-tier designers leave the market due to image-generating AI, while high-tier designers become more engaged. \cite{xue2022college} find that increased AI positively affects non-academically trained workers' employment but negatively impacts academically trained employees. 

As the capabilities of GenAI overlap more with those of humans, the integration of human work with GenAI poses new fundamental challenges and opportunities, in particular in knowledge-intensive firms and organizations. Understanding the implications of GenAI for organizations and individuals has taken on urgency among scholars, workers, companies, and even governments \citep{brynjolfsson2023generative, zhang2024college, capraro2024impact}. Recent studies of GenAI suggest that performance increases from using GenAI, especially for writing tasks \citep{NoyShakked2023Eeot} and programming \citep{peng2023impact}, consulting tasks \citep{dell2023navigating}, as well as for ideation and creative work \citep{girotra2023ideas}. As a result, the effects of GenAI are expected to be higher on the most creative, highly paid, and highly educated workers \citep{felten2023occupational, eloundou2024gpts}. \cite{agrawal2023turing} argue that AI-driven task automation can actually enhance job prospects, raise labor income, and potentially reduce inequality by expanding the skill value of many workers---a concept they term the ``Turing Transformation."  Building on this literature, we employ a formal analytical model of knowledge-based hierarchies to systematically examine the premises, drivers, and outcomes of GenAI adoption in knowledge-intensive organizations. Our approach is distinct in its focus on the organizational design implications of GenAI, particularly as it pertains to workforce dynamics and skill hierarchies.

Our paper also contributes to the recently emerging literature stream on the economics of GenAI \citep[e.g.,][]{acemoglu2024simple, xu2024economics}. With the popularity of ChatGPT, many scholars have engaged in research on its social and economic impacts in a wide range of fields, such as marketing \citep{brand2023using, goli2024frontiers}, finance \citep{lopez2023can,eisfeldt2024labor,eisfeldt2024ai}, healthcare \citep{thirunavukarasu2023large, moor2023foundation}, and so on. Our study approaches this question from a different angle. To our knowledge, this paper is the first modeling study to examine GenAI's workforce implications through the lens of organizational structure. By doing so, we aim to fill a critical gap in the literature and provide a framework for understanding the transformative role of GenAI in the knowledge economy.
