\section{Human-in-the-Loop Validation}\label{section-validation}
The preceding analysis has shown that the hallucination rate is a pivotal factor in the adoption of GenAI, as reducing hallucination risks can significantly enhance firm profitability. However, hallucinations are not simply implementation flaws but are intrinsic to generative language models, arising regardless of model architecture, data quality, or scale \citep{kalai2024calibrated}. This challenge manifests across various professional domains with serious consequences. In legal services, for example, GenAI systems can fabricate legal precedents or misinterpret statutory requirements, potentially jeopardizing client interests and damaging firms' professional credibility. In healthcare, a recent study highlights that large language models often produce hallucinations in medical summaries, even among advanced models like OpenAI's GPT-4o and Meta's Llama-3 \citep{hatem2023call}. These cases underscore the severe professional and reputational risks posed by hallucinations in fields where accuracy is essential.

In response, organizations are increasingly adopting human-in-the-loop validation, where managers review GenAI-assisted outputs before they are finalized. This layer of manager validation ensures that GenAI-assisted workflows maintain high levels of accuracy and reliability. By combining the efficiency of AI with human judgment, this approach enhances the dependability of outputs---a practice that is especially critical in high-stakes sectors. In legal practices, for example, law firms are establishing structured review protocols in which senior attorneys validate AI-generated research and draft documents.\footnote{\href{https://www.clio.com/resources/ai-for-lawyers/law-firm-ai-policy/}{https://www.clio.com/resources/ai-for-lawyers/law-firm-ai-policy/}} Similarly, in healthcare, human oversight of AI systems is crucial to avoid misdiagnoses or inappropriate treatment recommendations, both of which carry potentially severe consequences. 

In this section, we assess the effects of GenAI advancements under human-in-the-loop validation, where managers are responsible for reviewing and verifying tasks completed by workers utilizing GenAI. Our analysis further explores how this additional layer of manager validation impacts both organizational structure and operational efficiency. We use the subscript $v$ to denote scenarios where human-in-the-loop validation is integrated.

