Proposition \ref{prop-validation-r}(b) shows that when the hallucination rate falls below a specific threshold (i.e., $h<h_3$), reductions in hallucination rates lead to an increase in the span of control (see Figure \ref{fig-validation-nm-t}). At low hallucination rates, workers become more reliant on GenAI for problem-solving, handling problems across a wider range. In this context, the time saved by managers on post-validation corrections due to reduced hallucinations outweighs the increased managerial support needed from lowering worker knowledge levels. As a result, the span of control expands as hallucination rates decrease. This finding has important implications for organizational dynamics and stakeholder interests (see Table \ref{table-summary-validation} for a summary). Manager validation fundamentally changes how hallucination reduction affects organizational structure: when hallucination rates are already low, further improvements can actually reduce the need for managers. This creates a significant misalignment: once hallucination rates fall below the critical threshold, the interests of firms and managers diverge regarding further improvements in GenAI reliability. 

Following Proposition \ref{prop-validation-r}, we can also determine the effect of GenAI adoption on the span of control in the case with manager validation (details in Proposition \ref{coro-3}). In contrast to scenarios without validation, where a high hallucination rate tends to increase the span of control (as shown in Figure \ref{fig-nocheck-ben-nm}), with manager validation, a reduction in the span of control is more likely when the hallucination rate is high (see Figure \ref{fig-check-ben-nm.pdf}). This is because a high hallucination rate increases the likelihood that tasks completed by workers with GenAI will fail the validation. As a result, managers must spend additional time assisting workers with problem-solving after a failed validation, which reduces the span of control.

\subsection{Impact of Human-in-the-Loop Validation}
We next examine three critical questions through systematic comparison of validation and non-validation scenarios: (1) How does human-in-the-loop validation influence firms' decisions to adopt GenAI? (2) What impact does it have on operational efficiency and firm profitability? (3) How does it transform organizational structures, particularly regarding worker knowledge requirements and the span of control? Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact} illustrates the impacts of human-in-the-loop validation under different parameter regions, with detailed conditions provided in Appendix \ref{app-validation}.


\begin{proposition}\label{proposition-validation-adoption}
 In Region I of Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}, human-in-the-loop validation discourages the adoption of GenAI; however, it encourages adoption in Region II.


\end{proposition}

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{figure/fig-validation-impact.pdf}
	\caption{Impact of human-in-the-loop validation  ($k=0.6$, $w=0.3$, $t_c=0.8$, $r=0.2$)}
	\label{fig-validation-impact}
\end{figure}

Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-adoption} reveals that human-in-the-loop validation can create contrasting effects on GenAI adoption depending on the relationship between hallucination rates and validation costs. In scenarios with low hallucination rates but high validation costs (Region I in Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}), validation actually discourages GenAI adoption. When firms require validation to ensure output reliability, excessive validation costs can make GenAI adoption economically unfeasible, even though the firm might benefit from GenAI if it could tolerate some hallucinations. The deterrent effect of validation becomes more pronounced as hallucination rates increase. Higher rates of hallucination mean that AI-generated outputs fail to meet standards more frequently, requiring more managerial intervention for post-validation problem-solving. This escalating demand for managerial oversight makes GenAI adoption increasingly unprofitable under validation requirements. However, this dynamic reverses when hallucination rates are high but validation costs are low (Region II in Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}). In these cases, firms can effectively mitigate significant output losses through relatively modest validation investments, making GenAI adoption beneficial. Yet even in this scenario, as hallucination rates rise, the costs of validation increase correspondingly. Consequently, validation only promotes GenAI adoption when validation costs remain below progressively lower thresholds as hallucination rates increase.

Apart from the two scenarios outlined in Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-adoption}, when both hallucination rates and validation costs are high (i.e., Region IV in Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}), GenAI offers no benefit to the firm, regardless of whether human-in-the-loop validation is implemented. Conversely, when both factors remain relatively low (i.e., Regions III.a, III.b, and III.c in Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}), GenAI consistently enhances firm value, making the presence of manager validation irrelevant to adoption decisions. Under these conditions of low hallucination rates and validation costs (i.e., $h<h_1$ and $t_v<t_{v1}$), we conduct a comparative analysis examining three key organizational metrics across validation and non-validation scenarios: firm profitability, worker knowledge requirements, and span of control.

\begin{proposition}\label{proposition-validation-compare}
\begin{enumerate}[$(a)$]
    \item Human-in-the-loop validation increases the firm's profit and lowers worker knowledge levels in Region III.a; otherwise, it reduces the firm's profit and increases worker knowledge levels in Regions III.b and III.c.

    \item Human-in-the-loop validation increases the span of control in Region III.c; otherwise, it decreases the span of control in Regions III.a and III.b.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-compare}(a) reveals that human-in-the-loop validation enhances firm profits and simultaneously reduces worker knowledge requirements only when validation costs remain relatively low (i.e., Region III.a in Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}). As hallucination rates increase, validation becomes more valuable to firms due to the higher potential output losses from hallucinations. This increasing value allows validation to remain profitable at higher cost levels, as evidenced by the rise in $t_{v2}$ with increasing $h$. In this low-cost validation scenario, validation actually reduces the effective cost of GenAI adoption by providing a more economical way to address hallucinations compared to their potential losses. This cost efficiency enables firms to employ workers with lower knowledge levels while expanding their use of GenAI. However, this relationship inverts when validation costs are high (i.e., Regions III.b and III.c in Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}). Under these conditions, validation becomes unprofitable, forcing firms to increase worker knowledge levels to limit GenAI usage to avoid excessive validation and post-validation communication costs.

One might expect human-in-the-loop validation to narrow the span of control, as managers must divide their time between validating AI-generated outputs and assisting workers, seemingly limiting their supervisory capacity. From managers' perspective, validation might appear advantageous, creating additional roles in both output validation and post-validation problem resolution. However, Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-compare}(b) reveals a counterintuitive outcome: when validation costs are high (i.e., Region III.c in Figure \ref{fig-validation-impact}), validation actually widens the span of control, reducing manager requirements. This occurs because high validation costs force firms to employ workers with higher knowledge levels to limit GenAI usage, thereby avoiding excessive validation and communication costs. Without validation requirements, firms can more freely employ workers with lower knowledge levels and rely more heavily on GenAI, leading to more frequent managerial consultations. Paradoxically, although validation might appear to create additional managerial roles through validation tasks and post-validation communication for problem-solving, it ultimately reduces managers' workload and increases their span of control.
