\section{The GenAI Productivity Bandwagon}\label{section-productivity}
Our analysis thus far has examined two distinctive features of GenAI technology: its capability level (the extent to which it augments workers' problem-solving abilities beyond their knowledge) and hallucination rate (the probability of generating plausible but incorrect outputs). In this section, we turn our attention to a third crucial aspect---productivity improvement---which, while not unique to GenAI, manifests in potentially distinctive ways within post-GenAI organizations. 

Recent empirical evidence suggests significant productivity benefits from GenAI adoption. Studies show consultants using AI completed 12.2\% more tasks and worked 25.1\% faster \citep{dell2023navigating}, while customer support agents saw a 14\% overall productivity increase, with novice workers gaining the most (35\% improvement) \citep{brynjolfsson2023generative}. \cite{cui2024effects} find that usage of a GenAI coding assistant increases software developer productivity by 26.08\%. \cite{capraro2024impact} provide an interdisciplinary overview of the probable impacts of GenAI, highlighting the potential to boost workplace productivity while creating new jobs, though benefits may be unevenly distributed. 

Industry projections are notably optimistic. McKinsey estimates GenAI could generate \$2.6-4.4 trillion annually across 63 use cases \citep{chui2023economic}, while Goldman Sachs forecasts a 1.5 percentage point increase in annual productivity growth over the next decade, potentially raising global GDP by 7 percent (\$7 trillion).\footnote{\href{https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/articles/generative-ai-could-raise-global-gdp-by-7-percent}{https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/articles/generative-ai-could-raise-global-gdp-by-7-percent}} Such optimistic projections are rooted in the concept of ``\textit{productivity bandwagon}": a widespread belief that technological progress inherently increases productivity, benefiting society as a whole. However, this perspective has been challenged by economists like Daron Acemoglu and Simon Johnson. In their book ``\textit{Power and Progress}," they argue that technological progress does not guarantee equitable prosperity, highlighting that the impact of technological advancements depends significantly on their implementation and ownership \citep{johnson2023power}. They advocate for technological development that complements human labor rather than replaces it, emphasizing the importance of distributing productivity gains equitably across society \citep{acemoglu2023rebalancing}.

Building on these insights, we examine how GenAI-driven productivity improvements uniquely affect organizational structures and earnings distribution. We particularly focus on how these productivity effects interact with GenAI's distinctive features---capability levels and hallucination rates. We model productivity improvement by assuming that after GenAI adoption, each worker can solve $A$ problems per unit of time, where $A>1$ represents worker productivity.\footnote{This aligns with the standard definition of productivity---average output per worker or total output divided by total employment. We focus on worker productivity improvement as empirical evidence suggests minimal productivity impacts on highly skilled workers such as managers \citep{brynjolfsson2023generative}.} When $A=1$, this model reduces to our baseline model from Section \ref{section-model}. Similar to traditional AI, workers can leverage GenAI to improve the efficiency of problem-solving, even when addressing tasks within their existing knowledge base. Notably, the productivity gains and the augmentation of workers' problem-solving capabilities facilitated by GenAI operate independently of each other. Under this productivity improvement, the firm's profit function becomes:
\begin{equation}\label{equ-productivity}
    \Pi_2(x)=1-hr(1-x)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right)\frac{1}{A}-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)\frac{1}{A}(1-r)(1-x)t_c.
\end{equation}

This formulation captures several key effects of productivity improvements. First, the firm now needs only $1/A$ workers to handle one problem per unit of time, reducing total worker wages to $(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2)\frac{1}{A}$. GenAI continues to assist workers with problems in the difficulty range $[x, x+r(1-x)]$, with hallucinations causing expected output losses of $hr(1-x)$. Second, for problems beyond both worker knowledge and GenAI capabilities, i.e., problems in $[x+r(1-x), 1]$, workers still require managerial assistance. However, the reduced workforce means managers spend less time on worker consultation, requiring only $\frac{1}{A}(1-r)(1-x)t_c$ managers. Consequently, total managerial wage costs become $(w+\frac{1}{2}k) \frac{1}{A}(1-r)(1-x)t_c$. 

\subsection{How Would Productivity Improvement Reshape Post-GenAI Organizations?}
The following proposition analyzes how this productivity improvement affects optimal GenAI adoption strategies and reshapes organizational structures. For technical details, we refer readers to Appendix \ref{app-productivity}.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop-productivity}
Considering GenAI-driven productivity improvement, the firm should adopt GenAI if and only if $A>A_1$.
\begin{enumerate}[$(a)$]

    \item When $A_1<A<A_2$, the optimal worker knowledge level and the span of control are  
    $$(x_2^*,s_2^*)=\left(\frac{(1-r)(k+2w)t_c+2hrA}{2k}, ~\frac{1}{(1-r)(1-x_2^*)t_c}\right).$$
    Moreover, both $x_2^*$ and $s_2^*$ increase in $A$.
    \item When $A\geq A_2$, the traditional two-layer organizational hierarchy collapses into a single-layer structure comprised solely of workers who hold complete knowledge, i.e., $x_2^*=1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Proposition \ref{prop-productivity} reveals how increasing productivity fundamentally reshapes organizational structure. When the productivity improvement exceeds a certain threshold ($A>A_1$), GenAI adoption becomes economically inevitable for firms, even in the presence of substantial hallucination risks. While hallucinations can cause substantial output losses through errors, sufficiently large productivity gains create an economic imperative that outweighs these costs. As productivity rises, the organization undergoes several interconnected changes. First, the reduced need for workers makes it economically viable to employ individuals with higher knowledge levels (i.e., an \textit{upskilling} effect), thereby minimizing losses from GenAI hallucinations. This shift toward more knowledgeable workers leads to higher individual wages, effectively narrowing the wage gap between organizational levels. Second, as workers become more knowledgeable, they require less managerial assistance, widening the span of control. 

Finally, when productivity surpasses a critical threshold ($A\ge A_2$), the optimal strategy shifts dramatically: firms employ workers with complete knowledge ($x_2^*=1$), effectively eliminating the need for managerial oversight. In this scenario, the two distinctive features of GenAI---knowledge augmentation and hallucination risk---become irrelevant, leaving productivity enhancement as its sole contribution. This transforms the traditional hierarchical structure into a single-layer organization composed entirely of highly knowledgeable workers who can independently handle all tasks. In essence, high-productivity GenAI environments catalyze a shift toward more egalitarian and less hierarchical organizational forms. In this new paradigm, traditional manager-level roles are redefined, evolving into specialized knowledge-worker positions. This flattened organizational structure maximizes efficiency by fully capitalizing on both the comprehensive expertise of the human workforce and the productivity enhancement offered by GenAI.

\subsection{What Is Unique about the GenAI-driven Productivity Improvement?}
To demonstrate that the shifts in workforce composition and organizational structure due to productivity gains, as outlined in Proposition \ref{prop-productivity}, are unique to GenAI adoption, we compare them with productivity gains from traditional technologies like information and communication technology (ICT), as in \citet{bloom2014distinct}. Unlike GenAI, traditional ICT merely reduces the number of workers needed per unit of time without expanding workers' problem-solving capabilities beyond their knowledge boundaries. Under ICT-driven productivity improvements, the firm's profit function becomes:
\begin{equation}\label{equ-productivity-noGenAI}
    \Pi_3(x)=1-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right)\frac{1}{A}-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)\frac{1}{A}(1-x)t_c.
\end{equation}
From this, we can directly derive that the optimal worker knowledge level is $\frac{(k+2w)t_c}{2k}$ and the optimal span of control is $\frac{2k}{[2k-(k+2w)t_c]t_c}$. 

