\begin{APPENDICES}
\section{Autonomous AI Agents}\label{section-extension2}
The evolution of GenAI has significantly enhanced its ability to emulate human roles within knowledge-based economies. This progression suggests a shift from human-augmented AI to AI systems that operate with human-like capabilities. To aid businesses in navigating this transformation, this appendix explores an advanced AI automation framework where GenAI, functioning as autonomous \textit{AI agents}, has the potential to fully replace employees or managers. We will analyze the optimal strategies for integrating GenAI in contexts where it functions with human-like proficiency. For ease of exposition, we refer the reader to the proofs of propositions for related technical details.

\subsection{Manager Displacement}
First, we consider the scenario in which the firm utilizes GenAI to displace managers. In such a case, workers solve problems that exceed their knowledge scope with the assistance of GenAI, and there is no need to consult managers (\textit{manager displacement case}). This scenario can be understood as GenAI's capability levels reaching the extreme level of $r=1$. The displacement of managers eliminates the previous communication costs incurred by managers when assisting workers. However, due to GenAI hallucinations, workers may not correctly solve problems with the help from AI agents. The firm's optimization problem remains to determine the knowledge level of workers to maximize its profit, i.e., 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\max}_x\;\Pi_4(x)=1-h(1-x)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right).
\end{equation*}
The worker independently solves problems within the difficulty range $[0, x]$ and, with the help of AI agents, addresses problems within $[x, 1]$. Due to GenAI hallucinations, the firm's expected output per unit of time decreases from 1 to $1-h(1-x)$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop-extention2-worker}
When GenAI can completely displace managers, the firm's optimal strategy for GenAI adoption is as follows.
\begin{enumerate}[$(a)$]
    \item When $h<h_1$, the firm should employ workers to solve problems within $[0, \frac{h}{k}]$ and use GenAI to assist workers for problems within $[\frac{h}{k}, 1]$;

    \item Otherwise, the firm should not adopt GenAI. 
\end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}

Intuitively, when the hallucination rate is not too high ($h<h_1$), the firm should allow the worker to handle the easiest problems independently and seek GenAI's help for others. Once the hallucination rate reaches or exceeds a certain threshold ($h\ge h_1$), the firm should abandon GenAI and revert to the traditional hierarchical structure consisting of workers and managers.

\subsection{Worker Displacement}
Next, we examine the scenario in which GenAI is used to displace workers. In this case, GenAI can directly engage in production and is capable of solving all potential problems, though GenAI hallucinations remain a concern. Now, the firm's optimization problem becomes determining the scope of problems that GenAI addresses independently, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\max}_y\;\Pi_5(y)=1-hy-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)(1-y)t_c.
\end{equation*}
Specifically, GenAI independently handles the problems within the difficulty range $[0, y]$, with a probability $h$ that the problem cannot be solved correctly due to hallucinations. For the problems within the range $[y, 1]$, they are addressed with the assistance of managers, ensuring that GenAI can always solve them correctly. We assume that when managers assist GenAI, they incur the same communication cost $t_c$ as when assisting workers. Therefore, the required number of managers is $(1-y)t_c$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop-extention2-manager}
    When GenAI can completely displace workers, the firm's optimal strategy for GenAI adoption is as follows.
    \begin{enumerate}[$(a)$]
        \item When $h<h_4$, the firm should utilize GenAI to handle all problems; 

        \item When $h\ge h_1$ and $t_c<t_{c1}$, the firm should employ managers to assist GenAI in solving all problems;

        \item Otherwise, the firm should not adopt GenAI.
    \end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}


As demonstrated in Proposition \ref{prop-extention2-manager}, there are two alternative adoption modes that could be optimal for the firm. One mode is for GenAI to handle all problems directly, eliminating the need for both workers and managers (\textit{autonomous AI} case). The other mode involves managers consistently assisting GenAI for problem-solving to avoid hallucinations (\textit{worker displacement} case). The choice between these two modes hinges on the communication cost between GenAI and managers, as well as the extent of GenAI hallucination rate. As long as the GenAI hallucination rate is sufficiently low ($h<h_4$), having GenAI directly handle all problems maximizes the firm's profit. However, if the hallucination rate is relatively high, the firm should only have managers assist GenAI in solving all problems when communication costs are low ($t_c<t_{c1}$); otherwise, displacing workers with GenAI becomes less cost-effective.



\subsection{A Holistic View}
Finally, we investigate the firm's optimal strategy for adopting GenAI when it has the potential to displace workers or managers. In this context, the firm needs to decide whether to have GenAI displace managers, displace workers, or adopt an autonomous AI approach. The following Proposition \ref{prop-aggressive-adoption} and Figure \ref{fig-region-aggressive} provide a holistic view in this human-like AI setting. 


\begin{proposition}\label{prop-aggressive-adoption}
When GenAI can completely displace workers or managers, the firm's optimal strategy for GenAI adoption is given in Table \ref{table-advance-AI}. 

