As shown in Proposition \ref{prop-aggressive-adoption}, when the hallucination rate is relatively low, the firm should fully rely on GenAI for problem-solving (Region I in Figure \ref{fig-region-aggressive}). This is because the savings from not employing any individuals outweigh the potential output losses caused by GenAI hallucinations. However, as the hallucination rate increases, tolerating the losses from GenAI hallucinations becomes uneconomical. When communication costs are relatively low, adopting a hierarchical structure where managers assist GenAI in solving all problems is optimal, as it eliminates losses from GenAI hallucinations while incurring relatively low communication costs (Region II in Figure \ref{fig-region-aggressive}). Conversely, as  communication costs between managers and GenAI rise, this strategy becomes less cost-effective. With high communication costs but a moderate hallucination rate, a hierarchical structure with workers and GenAI is preferable (Region III in Figure \ref{fig-region-aggressive}). In this case, the firm employs workers with lower knowledge levels to handle the simplest problems, while more complex problems are addressed with the help of GenAI. In other words, the firm seeks a balance between workers' knowledge-related compensation costs and output losses from GenAI hallucinations. Finally, if both communication costs and the hallucination rate are high, neither manager assistance nor worker involvement can effectively mitigate the negative impact of GenAI hallucinations, making it optimal for the firm to abandon GenAI (Region IV in Figure \ref{fig-region-aggressive}).


Our findings from Proposition \ref{prop-aggressive-adoption} and Figure \ref{fig-region-aggressive} suggest that advancing GenAI capabilities, coupled with declining hallucination rates, will catalyze organizational restructuring along two primary trajectories: either towards a streamlined single-layer architecture entirely operated by AI agents, or hierarchical structures where AI agents operate under manager's oversight, effectively supplanting human workers. This transition signals a potential fundamental shift in traditional workforce dynamics, with diminishing roles for conventional workers.

The corporate landscape of 2024 provides empirical support for our theoretical predictions regarding organizational transformation driven by GenAI adoption. Major U.S. corporations across technology, finance, manufacturing, and media sectors are implementing significant workforce reductions, with industry leaders like Meta, IBM, Google, and Microsoft, frequently citing AI integration and automation as key strategic drivers.\footnote{\href{https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/13/tech-layoffs-2024-list/}{https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/13/tech-layoffs-2024-list/}} This trend reflects a broader shift towards automation and efficiency amidst evolving economic landscapes.

Moreover, the continued reduction in GenAI hallucination rates points to an eventual end state where organizations progressively eliminate managerial positions, transitioning to systems of fully autonomous AI agents once hallucination rates fall below a critical threshold. This progression suggests a comprehensive transformation in organizational architecture, driven by advances in AI reliability and capability.
