\section{Model Robustness Check}\label{app-hallucination}
%\subsection{Model Robustness Check}
We examine two alternative scenarios concerning different forms of hallucination rates to verify the robustness of our main insights. First, we consider the case where hallucination rates increase with problem difficulty, assuming the hallucination rate follows $h(x)=gx$, where $0<g<1$. Second, we study the case where hallucination rates inversely correlate with GenAI capability levels, assuming $h=g(1-r)$.
\subsection{$h(x)=gx$}
In this subsection, we assume $h(x)=gx$ to examine the scenario in which hallucination rates increase with problem difficulty, where $0<g<1$ is the hallucination coefficient. After adopting GenAI, the firm's profit function becomes: 
\begin{equation*}
    \Pi_6(x)=1-\int_x^{x+r(1-x)}gzdz-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)(1-r)(1-x)t_c.
\end{equation*}
Here, the term $\int_x^{x+r(1-x)}gzdz$ denotes the firm's expected output losses caused by GenAI hallucinations as the worker solves problems falling within the range $[x, x+r(1-x)]$ with the assistance of GenAI. The remaining part of the firm's profit function remains the same as when the hallucination rate is exogenous.  

