\section{Proofs of Statements}
We present all the proofs of Lemmas and Propositions in this appendix.\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-noGenAI}}:\\
In the absence of GenAI, the firm's optimization problem is 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\max}_x\;\Pi_0(x)=1-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)(1-x)t_c.
\end{equation*}
Since $\frac{\partial^2\Pi_0}{\partial x^2}=-k<0$, $\Pi_0(x)$ is strictly concave in $x$. From the first-order condition, we can derive the optimal worker knowledge level $$x_0^*=\frac{(k+2w)t_c}{2k}.$$ 
Under the assumption $0<t_c<\overline{t}_c$, we have $0<x_0^*<1$. Plugging $x_0^*$ into $n_0$, $s_0$ and $\Pi_0$, we have that the optimal number of managers is $$n_0^*=(1-x_0^*)t_c=\frac{[2k-(k+2w)t_c]t_c}{2k},$$ 
the optimal span of control is 
$$s_0^*=\frac{1}{(1-x_0^*)t_c}=\frac{2k}{[2k-(k+2w)t_c]t_c},$$ 
and the firm's optimal profit is $$\Pi_0^*=\frac{[2k-(k+2w)t_c]^2+4k(2-k-2w)}{8k}.$$ 
This proves the result in Lemma \ref{lemma-noGenAI}.     \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-novalidation-equ}}:\\
If the firm adopts GenAI, the firm's optimization problem is 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\max}_x\;\Pi_1(x)=1-hr(1-x)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)(1-r)(1-x)t_c.
\end{equation*}
Since $\frac{\partial^2\Pi_1}{\partial x^2}=-k<0$, $\Pi_1(x)$ is strictly concave in $x$. Therefore, from the first-order condition, the unconstrained optimal knowledge level can be solved as 
$$x_1=\frac{(1-r)(k+2w)t_c+2hr}{2k}.$$
One can check that $x_1<1$ for $h<h_a$ and $x_1\ge 1$ for $h\ge h_a$, where 
$$h_a=\frac{2k-(1-r)(k+2w)t_c}{2r}.$$
Therefore, if the firm adopts GenAI, the firm's optimal knowledge level of workers is 
\begin{equation*}
     x_1^*
    =\left \{ 
    \begin{aligned}
    &\; 1, \quad\quad\quad && \text{if \;} h\ge h_a; \\
    &\; x_1, \quad\quad\quad && \text{o.w. \;}      \quad\quad\quad  \\
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
\end{equation*}
When $h\ge h_a$, it is straightforward to see $\Pi_1^*-\Pi_0^*=\Pi_1|_{x=1}-\Pi_0^*<0$, and thus the firm should not adopt GenAI. When $h<h_a$, by plugging $x_1^*=x_1$ into $\Pi_1$, we have 
\begin{equation*}
    \Pi_1^*-\Pi_0^*=\frac{r[(k+2w)t_c-2h][4k-(2-r)(k+2w)t_c-2rh]}{8k}.
\end{equation*}
One can check that $\Pi_1^*>\Pi_0^*$ for $h<h_1$ or $h>h_b$ and $\Pi_1^*\le\Pi_0^*$ for $h_1\le h\le h_b$, where 
$$h_1=\frac{(k+2w)t_c}{2}, ~~ h_b=\frac{4k-(2-r)(k+2w)t_c}{2r}.$$
We have $h_1<h_a<h_b$ under the assumption $0<t_c<\overline{t}_c$. Therefore, the firm should adopt GenAI (i.e., $\Pi_1^*>\Pi_0^*$) if and only if $h<h_1$, and the optimal worker knowledge level is $x_1^*=x_1$. Under the condition $h<h_1$, we have 
$$x_1^*-x_0^*=-\frac{r[(k+2w)t_c-2h]}{2k}<0, ~~ \frac{\partial x_1^*}{\partial r}=-\frac{[(k+2w)t_c-2h]}{2k}<0, ~~ \frac{\partial x_1^*}{\partial h}=\frac{r}{k}>0.$$
Plugging $x_1^*$ into $n_1$ and $\Pi_1$, we have that the optimal number of managers is
$$n_1^*=(1-r)(1-x_1^*)t_c=\frac{(1-r)[(kt_c+2wt_c-2h)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]t_c}{2k},$$
and the firm's optimal profit is 
$$\Pi_1^*=\frac{1}{8k}\Big\{[(kt_c+2wt_c-2h)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]^2+4k(2-k-2w)\Big\}.$$  
This proves the result in Proposition \ref{prop-novalidation-equ}.     \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-novalidation-rt}}:\\
When $h<h_1$, the firm adopts GenAI and the optimal span of control is 
$$s_1^*=\frac{1}{(1-r)(1-x_1^*)t_c}=\frac{2k}{(1-r)[(kt_c+2wt_c-2h)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]t_c}.$$
We have 
$$\frac{\partial s_1^*}{\partial r}=\frac{4k[k+h-(k+2w)t_c+(kt_c+2wt_c-2h)r]}{t_c[(kt_c+2wt_c-2h)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]^2(1-r)^2}.$$
One can check that $\frac{\partial s_1^*}{\partial r}<0$ for $r<r_1$ and $\frac{\partial s_1^*}{\partial r}>0$ for $r>r_1$, where $$r_1=\frac{(k+2w)t_c-k-h}{(k+2w)t_c-2h}.$$ 
One can check that under the condition $h<h_1$, $r_1<1$ consistently holds. Therefore, after GenAI adoption, there exists a threshold $r_1<1$ such that $s_1^*$ strictly decreases in $r$ on $(0, r_1)$; otherwise, $s_1^*$ strictly increases in $r$ on $(r_1, 1)$. 

\par Regarding the impact of changes in hallucination rates, we have
$$\frac{\partial s_1^*}{\partial h}=\frac{4kr}{t_c(1-r)[(kt_c+2wt_c-2h)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]^2}>0.$$
Therefore, $s_1^*$ strictly increases in $h$.    \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-validation-equ}}:\\
If the firm adopts GenAI with validation, the firm's optimization problem is 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\max}_x\;\Pi_v(x)=1-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)[r(1-x)(t_v+ht_c)+(1-r)(1-x)t_c].
\end{equation*}
Since $\frac{\partial^2\Pi_v}{\partial x^2}=-k<0$, $\Pi_v(x)$ is strictly concave in $x$. Therefore, from the first-order condition, the unconstrained optimal knowledge level of workers can be solved as
$$x_v=\frac{(k+2w)[rt_v+(1+rh-r)t_c]}{2k}.$$
One can check that $x_v<1$ for $h<h_c$ and $x_v\ge 1$ for $h\ge h_c$, where 
$$h_c=\frac{2k-(k+2w)[(1-r)t_c+rt_v]}{(k+2w)rt_c}.$$
Therefore, if the firm adopts GenAI with validation, the optimal worker knowledge level is 
\begin{equation*}
     x_v^*
    =\left \{ 
    \begin{aligned}
    &\; 1, \quad\quad\quad && \text{if \;} h\ge h_c; \\
    &\; x_v, \quad\quad\quad && \text{o.w. \;}      \quad\quad\quad  \\
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
\end{equation*}
When $h\ge h_c$, it is straightforward to see $\Pi_v^*=\Pi_v|_{x=1}<\Pi_0^*$, and thus the firm should not adopt GenAI with validation. When $h<h_c$, by plugging $x_v^*=x_v$ into $\Pi_v$, we have 
\begin{equation*}
    \Pi_v^*-\Pi_0^*=\frac{r(2w+k)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)[4k-(k+2w)(2t_c-rt_c+rt_v)-t_c(k+2w)rh]}{8k}.
\end{equation*}
One can check that $\Pi_v^*>\Pi_0^*$ for $h<h_2$ or $h>h_d$ and $\Pi_v^*\le\Pi_0^*$ for $h_2\le h\le h_d$, where 
$$h_2=1-\frac{t_v}{t_c}, ~~ h_d=\frac{4k-(k+2w)[(2-r)t_c+rt_v]}{(k+2w)rt_c}.$$
We have $h_2<h_c<h_d$ under the assumption $0<t_c<\overline{t}_c$. Therefore, the firm should adopt GenAI with validation (i.e., $\Pi_v^*>\Pi_0^*$) if and only if $h<h_2$, and the optimal worker knowledge level is $x_v^*=x_v$. Under the condition $h<h_2$, we have 
$$x_v^*-x_0^*=-\frac{r(k+2w)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)}{2k}<0,$$
$$\frac{\partial x_v^*}{\partial r}=-\frac{(k+2w)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)}{2k}<0, ~~ \frac{\partial x_v^*}{\partial h}=\frac{(k+2w)rt_c}{2k}>0.$$
Plugging $x_v^*$ into $n_v$ and $\Pi_v$, we have that the optimal number of managers is 
\begin{align*}
    n_v^*=&r(1-x_v^*)(t_v+ht_c)+(1-r)(1-x_v^*)t_c\\[2mm]
    =&\frac{1}{2k}[t_c-r(t_c-t_v-ht_c)][(2w+k)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c],
\end{align*}
%$$n_v^*=r(1-x_v^*)(t_v+ht_c)+(1-r)(1-x_v^*)t_c=\frac{[t_c-r(t_c-t_v-ht_c)][(2w+k)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]}{2k},$$ 
and the firm's optimal profit is 
$$\Pi_v^*=\frac{1}{8k}\{[(2w+k)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]^2+4k(2-k-2w)\}.$$ 

\par This gives the results in Proposition \ref{prop-validation-equ}.      \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-validation-r}}:\\
When $h<h_2$, the firm adopts GenAI with validation and the optimal span of control is 
\begin{align*}
    s_v^*=&\frac{1}{r(1-x_v^*)(t_v+ht_c)+(1-r)(1-x_v^*)t_c}  \\[2mm]
         =&\frac{2k}{[t_c-r(t_c-t_v-ht_c)][(2w+k)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]}.
\end{align*}
We have 
$$\frac{\partial s_v^*}{\partial r}=\frac{4k(t_c-t_v-ht_c)[k-(k+2w)t_c+(k+2w)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)r]}{[t_c-r(t_c-t_v-ht_c)]^2[(2w+k)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]^2}.$$
$\frac{\partial s_v^*}{\partial r}<0$ for $r<r_2$ and $\frac{\partial s_v^*}{\partial r}>0$ for $r>r_2$, where $$r_2=\frac{(k+2w)t_c-k}{(k+2w)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)}.$$ Therefore, there exists a threshold $r_2$ such that $s_v^*$ strictly decreases in $r$ on $(0, r_2)$ and strictly increases in $r$ on $(r_2, 1)$.

Regarding the impact of changes in hallucination rates, we have 
$$\frac{\partial s_v^*}{\partial h}=\frac{4krt_c[rt_c(k+2w)h+(k+2w)t_c-k-r(k+2w)(t_c-t_v)]}{[t_c-r(t_c-t_v-ht_c)]^2[(2w+k)(t_c-t_v-ht_c)r+2k-(k+2w)t_c]^2}.$$
$\frac{\partial s_v^*}{\partial h}<0$ for $h<h_3$ and $\frac{\partial s_v^*}{\partial h}>0$ for $h>h_3$, where $$h_3=\frac{r(k+2w)(t_c-t_v)-(k+2w)t_c+k}{r(k+2w)t_c}.$$ 
Therefore, there exists a threshold $h_3$ such that $s_v^*$ strictly decreases in $h$ on $(0, h_3)$ and strictly increases in $h$ on $(h_3, h_2)$.    \Halmos\\[3mm] 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-adoption}}:\\
The firm adopts GenAI with validation if and only if $h<h_2$. The condition $h<h_2$ can be rewritten as $t_v<t_{v1}$, where $t_{v1}=(1-h)t_c$. Given that, in the absence of validation, the firm adopts GenAI if and only if $h<h_1$, we can directly derive Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-adoption}.   \Halmos\\[3mm] 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-compare}}:\\
Under the conditions $h<h_1$ and $t_v<t_{v1}$, the firm consistently adopts GenAI regardless of whether there is manager validation. Comparing the firm's optimal profits with and without validation, we have   
$$\Pi_v^*-\Pi_1^*=-\frac{r}{8k}G_1G_2,$$ where
$$G_1=-(k+2w)t_v+h[2-(k+2w)t_c],$$
$$G_2=r(k+2w)t_v+(rh-2r+2)(k+2w)t_c+2rh-4k.$$
When $t_v<t_{v1}$,
\begin{align*}
    G_2<&r(k+2w)(1-h)t_c+(rh-2r+2)(k+2w)t_c+2rh-4k \\
    =&(2-r)(2w+k)t_c+2rh-4k.
\end{align*}
$(2-r)(2w+k)t_c+2rh-4k>0$ is equivalent to $h>h_b$, where 
$$h_b=\frac{4k-(2-r)(k+2w)t_c}{2r}.$$
One can check that $h_1<h_b$ under the assumption $0<t_c<\overline{t}_c$. Therefore, under the conditions $h<h_1$ and $t_v<t_{v1}$, $G_2<0$ and $\Pi_v^*-\Pi_1^*>0$ is equivalent to $G_1>0$, which can be rewritten as $t_v<t_{v2}$, where $$t_{v2}=\frac{h[2-(k+2w)t_c]}{k+2w}.$$ 
Therefore, $\Pi_v^*>\Pi_1^*$ for $h<h_1$ and $t_v<t_{v2}$; $\Pi_v^*<\Pi_1^*$ for $h<h_1$ and $t_{v2}<t_v<t_{v1}$. Comparing the optimal worker knowledge levels with and without validation, we have 
$$x_v^*-x_1^*=-\frac{r}{2k}G_1.$$
Based on the discussion above, we can derive that $x_v^*<x_1^*$ for $h<h_1$ and $t_v<t_{v2}$; $x_v^*>x_1^*$ for $h<h_1$ and $t_{v2}<t_v<t_{v1}$. This gives Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-compare}(a). 

We then compare the optimal span of control with and without validation under the conditions $h<h_1$ and $t_v<t_{v1}$. Since $x_v^*<x_1^*$ for $t<t_{v2}$, we have 
$$s_v^*=\frac{1}{r(1-x_v^*)(ht_c+t_v)+(1-r)(1-x_v^*)t_c}<\frac{1}{(1-r)(1-x_v^*)t_c}<\frac{1}{(1-r)(1-x_1^*)t_c}=s_1^*.$$
Therefore, $s_v^*<s_1^*$ for $h<h_1$ and $t<t_{v2}$. When $h<h_1$ and $t_{v2}\le t_v<t_{v1}$, we need to directly compare $s_v^*$ and $s_1^*$. 
$$s_v^*-s_1^*=\frac{rs_1^*s_v^*G_3}{2k},$$
where 
\begin{align*}
    G_3=&r(k+2w)t_v^2+[2(1+rh-r)(k+2w)t_c-2k]t_v+h[(rh+2-2r)(k+2w)t_c-2k+2r-2]t_c.
\end{align*}
We have 
$$G_3|_{t_v=t_{v2}}=-\frac{2h}{2w+k}[2k-(1-r)(k+2w)t_c-2rh].$$
One can check that $G_3|_{t_v=t_{v2}}<0$ consistently holds under the conditions $h<h_1$ and $t_c<\overline{t}_c$. Therefore, $G_3>0$ for $t_{v3}<t<t_{v1}$ and $G_3<0$ for $t_{v2}\le t< t_{v3}$, where $t_v=t_{v3}$ is the larger root of the equation $s_v^*=s_1^*$. Combining the discussion above, we have that $s_v^*>s_1^*$ for $h<h_1$ and $t_{v3}<t_v<t_{v1}$; $s_v^*<s_1^*$ for $h<h_1$ and $t_v<\min\{t_{v1},t_{v3}\}$. This gives Proposition \ref{proposition-validation-compare}(b).    \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-productivity}}:\\
In the presence of productivity improvements, if the firm adopts GenAI, the firm's optimization problem becomes:
$$\mathop{\max}_x\;\Pi_2(x)=1-hr(1-x)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right)\frac{1}{A}-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)\frac{1}{A}(1-r)(1-x)t_c.$$
Since $\frac{\partial^2\Pi_2}{\partial x^2}=-\frac{k}{A}<0$, $\Pi_2(x)$ is strictly concave in $x$. Therefore, from the first-order condition, the unconstrained optimal knowledge level of workers can be solved as
$$x_2=\frac{(1-r)(k+2w)t_c+2hrA}{2k}.$$
One can check that $x_2<1$ for $A<A_2$ and $x_2\ge 1$ for $A\ge A_2$, where 
$$A_2=\frac{2k-(1-r)(k+2w)t_c}{2hr}.$$
Therefore, if the firm adopts GenAI, the optimal worker knowledge level is 
\begin{equation*}
     x_2^*
    =\left \{ 
    \begin{aligned}
    &\; x_2, \quad\quad\quad && \text{if \;}  A<A_2; \\
    &\; 1, \quad\quad\quad && \text{if \;}      A\ge A_2.  \\
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
\end{equation*}
One can check that $\Pi_2^*$ strictly increases in $A$ for $A>0$ and $\Pi_2^*\rightarrow -\infty$ as $A\rightarrow 0$. Therefore, there exists a unique positive solution $A=A_0$ to the equation $\Pi_2^*=\Pi_0^*$. Furthermore, $\Pi_2^*>\Pi_0^*$ for $A>A_0$ and $\Pi_2^*\le\Pi_0^*$ for $0<A\le A_0$. Since we focus on the case $A>1$ due to GenAI-driven productivity improvements, we have that the firm should adopt GenAI if and only if $A>A_1$, where $A_1=\max\{A_0, 1\}$. Consequently, after GenAI adoption, if $A_1<A<A_2$, the optimal worker knowledge level and the span of control are 
$$x_2^*=\frac{(1-r)(k+2w)t_c+2hrA}{2k}, ~~ s_2^*=\frac{1}{(1-r)(1-x_2^*)t_c}.$$
Otherwise, the optimal worker knowledge level is $x_2^*=1$, and thus there are no managers within the organization.    \Halmos\\[3mm] 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-productivity-welfare}}:\\
After GenAI adoption, the worker (total) income is defined as
$$W_2=\frac{1}{A}\left[w+\frac{1}{2}k(x_2^*)^2\right].$$
Since $x_2^*=1$ for $A\ge A_2$, $W_2$ strictly decreases in $A$ under this condition. We then consider the case $A_1<A<A_2$. We have 
$$\frac{\partial W_2}{\partial A}=\frac{1}{8kA^2}[4r^2h^2A^2-(1-r)^2(k+2w)^2t_c^2-8wk].$$
Therefore, $W_2$ strictly decreases in $A$ for $A_1<A<A_3$ and strictly increases in $A$ for $A_3<A<A_2$, where 
$$A_3=\frac{\sqrt{(1-r)^2(k+2w)^2t_c^2+8wk}}{2rh}.$$
This proves the result in Proposition \ref{prop-productivity-welfare}.    \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-productivity-interact}}:\\
Under the condition $A_1<A<A_2$, the optimal worker knowledge level is 
$$x_2^*=\frac{(1-r)(k+2w)t_c+2hrA}{2k},$$ which gives
$$\frac{\partial x_2^*}{\partial r}=\frac{2hA-(k+2w)t_c}{2k}.$$  
Therefore, $\frac{\partial x_2^*}{\partial r}<0$ for $A_1<A<A_4$ and $\frac{\partial x_2^*}{\partial r}>0$ for $A_4<A<A_2$, where $$A_4=\frac{(k+2w)t_c}{2h}.$$ 

\par Furthermore, $\frac{\partial x_2^*}{\partial h}=\frac{rA}{k}$ and $\frac{\partial x_1^*}{\partial h}=\frac{r}{k}$. Therefore, under the assumption $A>1$, $\frac{\partial x_2^*}{\partial h}>\frac{\partial x_1^*}{\partial h}$.   \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-extention2-worker}}:\\
When GenAI completely displaces managers, the firm's optimization problem is 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\max}_x\;\Pi_4(x)=1-h(1-x)-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right).
\end{equation*}
Since $\frac{\partial^2\Pi_4}{\partial x^2}=-k<0$, $\Pi_4(x)$ is strictly concave in $x$. Therefore, from the first-order condition, the unconstrained optimal knowledge level of workers can be solved as $x_4=\frac{h}{k}$. One can check that $x_4<1$ for $h<k$ and $x_4\ge 1$ for $h\ge k$. Therefore, if the firm adopts GenAI to displace managers, the optimal worker knowledge level is 
\begin{equation*}
     x_4^*
    =\left \{ 
    \begin{aligned}
    &\; 1, \quad\quad\quad && \text{if \;} h\ge k; \\
    &\; x_4, \quad\quad\quad && \text{o.w. \;}      \quad\quad\quad  \\
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
\end{equation*}
It is straightforward to see that when $h\ge k$, $\Pi_4^*=\Pi_4|_{x=1}<\Pi_0^*$, and thus the firm should not adopt GenAI. When $h<k$, by plugging $x_4^*=x_4$ into $\Pi_4$, we have  
$$\Pi_4^*-\Pi_0^*=\frac{1}{8k}[2h-(k+2w)t_c][(k+2w)t_c+2h-4k].$$
Under the condition $h<k$, we have $(k+2w)t_c+2h-4k<(k+2w)t_c-2k<0$. Therefore, $\Pi_4^*>\Pi_0^*$ is equivalent to $h<h_1$, where $$h_1=\frac{(k+2w)t_c}{2}<k.$$ 
Consequently, the firm should adopt GenAI to replace managers if and only if $h<h_1$ and the optimal worker knowledge level is $x_4^*=\frac{h}{k}$.     \Halmos\\[3mm]
%Combining the discussions above gives Proposition \ref{prop-extention2-worker}.    
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-extention2-manager}}:\\
When GenAI completely displaces workers, the firm's optimization problem is 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\max}_y\;\Pi_5(y)=1-hy-\left(w+\frac{1}{2}k\right)(1-y)t_c.
\end{equation*}
Since the firm's profit function can be rewritten as  
$$\Pi_5(y)=\frac{[(k+2w)t_c-2h]y+2-(k+2w)t_c}{2},$$
We can derive the firm's optimal choice as 
\begin{equation*}
     y^*
    =\left \{ 
    \begin{aligned}
    &\; 1, \quad\quad\quad && \text{if \;} h<h_1; \\
    &\; 0, \quad\quad\quad && \text{if \;} h\ge h_1.      \quad\quad\quad  \\
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
\end{equation*}

When $h<h_1$, $\Pi_5^*=\Pi_5|_{y=1}=1-h$. We have
$$\Pi_5^*-\Pi_0^*=\frac{1}{8k}[(k+2w)(4k-kt_c-2wt_c)t_c+8wk-8kh].$$
Therefore, $\Pi_5^*>\Pi_0^*$ for $h<h_4$ and $\Pi_5^*<\Pi_0^*$ for $h_4<h<h_1$, where $$h_4=\min\left\{\frac{(k+2w)(4k-kt_c-2wt_c)t_c+8wk}{8k}, h_1\right\}.$$
Consequently, the firm should utilize GenAI to handle all problems if and only if $h<h_4$. 
%$h<\min\{h_1, h_6\}$ and $\Pi_5^*<\Pi_0^*$ for $h_a<h<h_1$, where $$h_a=\frac{(k+2w)(4k-kt_c-2wt_c)t_c+8wk}{8k}. $$

When $h\ge h_1$, $\Pi_5^*=\Pi_5|_{y=0}=1-\frac{1}{2}(k+2w)t_c$. We have 
$$\Pi_5^*-\Pi_0^*=\frac{8kw-(k+2w)^2t_c^2}{8k}.$$
Therefore, $\Pi_5^*>\Pi_0^*$ for $h\ge h_1$ and $t_c<t_{c1}$, and $\Pi_5^*<\Pi_0^*$ for $h\ge h_1$ and $t_c>t_{c1}$, where $$t_{c1}=\frac{2\sqrt{2wk}}{k+2w}.$$ 
Consequently, the firm should employ managers to assist GenAI in solving all problems if and only if $h\ge h_1$ and $t_c<t_{c1}$.

Combining the discussions above gives Proposition \ref{prop-extention2-manager}.   \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\noindent\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop-aggressive-adoption}}:\\ 
Based on Propositions \ref{prop-extention2-worker} and \ref{prop-extention2-manager}, we can directly conclude that when $h\ge h_1$:
\begin{enumerate}[$(a)$]
    \item The firm should employ managers to assist GenAI in solving all problems (i.e., worker displacement) if $t_c<t_{c1}$;

    \item The firm should never adopt GenAI if $t_c\ge t_{c1}$.
\end{enumerate}
Additionally, when $h_4\le h<h_1$, the firm should employ workers to solve problems within $\left[0, \frac{h}{k}\right]$ and use GenAI to assist workers for problems within $\left[\frac{h}{k}, 1\right]$ (i.e., manager displacement). 

When $h<h_4$, we need to compare $\Pi_4^*|_{x_4^*=\frac{h}{k}}$ and $\Pi_5^*|_{y^*=1}$. We have 
$$\Pi_4^*|_{x_4^*=\frac{h}{k}}-\Pi_5^*|_{y^*=1}=\frac{h^2-2kw}{2k}.$$
Therefore, $\Pi_4^*|_{x_4^*=\frac{h}{k}}\ge\Pi_5^*|_{y^*=1}$ for $h_5\le h<h_4$ and $\Pi_5^*|_{y^*=1}>\Pi_4^*|_{x_4^*=\frac{h}{k}}$ for $h<\min\{h_4, h_5\}$, where $h_5=\sqrt{2kw}$. 
The firm should utilize GenAI to handle all problems (i.e., autonomous AI) if $h<\min\{h_4, h_5\}$. One can check that under the assumption $t_c<\overline{t}_c$, the condition $h<\min\{h_4, h_5\}$ can be rewritten as $h<\min\{h_1, h_5\}$. Furthermore, combining the conditions of $h_5\le h<h_4$ and $h_4\le h<h_1$, we have that the firm should choose manager displacement when $h_5\le h<h_1$. 

Combining the discussions above gives Proposition \ref{prop-aggressive-adoption}.   \Halmos\\[3mm]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
