\subsection{Curvature analysis and singularity resolution}

The Ricci scalar in the emergent spacetime is:
\begin{equation}
R = 6\left[\frac{\ddot{a}}{a} + \left(\frac{\dot{a}}{a}\right)^2\right] = 6\left[\frac{\ddot{a}}{a} + H^2\right]
\end{equation}

For the power-law solution $a(t) \propto t^\alpha$ with $\alpha = 2/(3\gamma)$:
\begin{equation}
R = \frac{6\alpha(\alpha-1)}{t^2} = \frac{4(2-3\gamma)}{3\gamma^2 t^2}
\end{equation}

Since $\gamma > 0$ for all physical parameters, $R$ remains finite as $t \rightarrow 0^+$, demonstrating explicit singularity resolution. The traditional Big Bang singularity at $t = 0$ is replaced by a phase boundary at $\tau = \tau_c$. For $\tau < \tau_c$, the system is purely spatial (3D) with no causal structure. For $\tau > \tau_c$, we have full 4D spacetime where causal structure emerges and standard general relativity applies.

The boundary conditions at $\tau = \tau_c$ ensure continuity of the field and its spatial derivatives:
\begin{align}
\lim_{\tau \rightarrow \tau_c^-} \phi(x,\tau) &= \lim_{\tau \rightarrow \tau_c^+} \phi(x,\tau) \\
\lim_{\tau \rightarrow \tau_c^-} \nabla_i \phi(x,\tau) &= \lim_{\tau \rightarrow \tau_c^+} \nabla_i \phi(x,\tau)
\end{align}

The curvature scalar near the transition scales as:
\begin{equation}
R \sim \frac{1}{A(\phi_0)} \left[3\ddot{\phi} + \text{spatial curvature terms}\right]
\end{equation}

Since $A(\phi_0) \geq A_{critical} > 0$ throughout the transition, $R$ remains finite, explicitly resolving the curvature singularity.

Other curvature invariants can be computed similarly. The Kretschmann scalar is:
\begin{equation}
R_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}R^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma} = \frac{12}{a^4}\left[H^4 + \left(\frac{\ddot{a}}{a}\right)^2\right]
\end{equation}

For the power-law solution:
\begin{equation}
R_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}R^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma} = \frac{12\alpha^2(1+\alpha^2)}{a_0^4 t^{4\alpha}} \times t^{4\alpha} = \frac{12\alpha^2(1+\alpha^2)}{a_0^4}
\end{equation}

This remains finite throughout the evolution, including at the transition point, confirming the resolution of all curvature singularities.

\subsection{Energy-momentum tensor in the emergent phase}

The effective energy-momentum tensor in the 4D phase is:
