Pretrained language models (PLMs) have achieved great success in deciphering the complex language of single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data \cite{geneformer,cui2024scgpt,hao2024large,yang2022scbert}, validating their capabilities fine-tuning for a variety of downstream tasks.
% GET \cite{fu2025foundation} integrates scATAC-seq and scRNA-seq data and builds a foundation model capable of uncovering regulatory grammars across cell types. However, it models scATAC-seq and scRNA-seq data in a pseudo-bulked format, leading to the limitation of cellular analysis. 
Nevertheless, the development of generalizable foundation models for scATAC-seq remains under-explored, posing significant challenges for cellular representation learning and multi-task transfer. Here, we identify and discuss three major challenges.

% (1) First, \textbf{scATAC-seq data are inherently high-dimensional and extremely sparse}. A single cell may span over a million OCRs, yet typically less than 1\% of these OCRs exhibit accessibility. This extreme sparsity poses substantial challenges to transformer-only architectures commonly used in transcriptomic foundation models \cite{geneformer,cui2024scgpt,hao2024large,wen2023cellplm,fu2025foundation,yang2022scbert}. Although some approaches attempt to mitigate the impact of high dimension and extreme sparsity by transforming accessibility into gene activity \cite{granja2021archr, stuart2021single}, filtering highly variable OCRs \cite{granja2021archr,tayyebi2024scalable,ghazanfar2024stabilized}, and accessibility binarization \cite{hao2021integrated, cusanovich2015multiplex,yuan2022scbasset}, such strategies often lead to substantial information loss \cite{su2025scmultimap,martens2024modeling,chen2019assessment}.

(1). \textbf{scATAC-seq data are inherently high-dimensional and sparse}, with single cells spanning over a million OCRs but typically less than 1\% showing accessibility. This sparsity complicates the utilization of Transformer~\cite{vaswani2017attention} adopted in transcriptomics~\cite{geneformer,cui2024scgpt,hao2024large,wen2023cellplm,fu2025foundation,yang2022scbert}. Other methods such as transforming accessibility into gene activity~\cite{granja2021archr, stuart2021single}, filtering highly variable OCRs~\cite{granja2021archr,tayyebi2024scalable,ghazanfar2024stabilized}, or binarizing accessibility~\cite{hao2021integrated, cusanovich2015multiplex,yuan2022scbasset} always cause substantial information loss \cite{su2025scmultimap,martens2024modeling,chen2019assessment}.

(2). \textbf{scATAC-seq data are not formatted in a standardized feature space}. Dynamic chromatin landscapes across diverse sources and platforms make dictionary-based tokenization for genes and nucleotides unsuitable for OCRs. All deep learning models for scATAC-seq \cite{xiong2019scale,xiong2022online,cui2024discrete,yuan2022scbasset} require dataset-specific training, leading to poor zero-shot performance.

% (2) Second, \textbf{scATAC-seq data derived from various sources and platforms may not possess a well-defined feature space}. Dynamic chromatin landscapes determined by peak calling \cite{zhang2008model} render dictionary-based tokenization methods suitable for genes inapplicable to OCRs in scATAC-seq data. Most existing models based on deep learning for scATAC-seq data \cite{xiong2019scale,xiong2022online,cui2024discrete,yuan2022scbasset} require dataset-specific training and lack prior biological knowledge, resulting in poor zero-shot performance.

(3). \textbf{current methods lack a unified integration of genomic information with chromatin accessibility profiles}. Both genomic information defining regulatory loci and chromatin accessibility profiles revealing their activity are essential in scATAC-seq data. However, VAE-based models \cite{xiong2019scale,xiong2022online,cui2024discrete} process accessibility matrices independently, while models like scBasset, CellSpace, and SANGO \cite{yuan2022scbasset,tayyebi2024scalable,zeng2024deciphering} predict binary accessibility from genomic sequences. These methods struggle to generalize across diverse downstream tasks.

% (3) Third, \textbf{ current architectures that model scATAC-seq lack a unified integration of genomic information with chromatin accessibility profiles}. Both genomic information defining regulatory loci and chromatin accessibility profiles revealing their activity are essential in scATAC-seq data. Models based on the variational autoencoder (VAE) framework \cite{xiong2019scale,xiong2022online,cui2024discrete} take the accessibility matrix as input independently. Models such as scBasset \cite{yuan2022scbasset}, CellSpace \cite{tayyebi2024scalable} and SANGO \cite{zeng2024deciphering} incorporate genomic sequence features to predict binary accessibility. All of these models built on such architectures are not able to generalize well to diverse downstream applications.

To address these challenges, we present \textbf{ChromFound}, a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. Its hybrid architecture integrates a Mamba block for efficient long-range sequence processing with a self-attention block to capture local regulatory dependencies within ±200 kb of transcriptional start sites, aligning with the typical range of enhancer-promoter interactions identified by Hi-C analysis~\cite{Bonev2016OrganizationAF}. ChromFound incorporates biologically informed OCR tokenization encoding genomic coordinates and non-binary accessibility profiles. This component ensures scalability and alignment across diverse scATAC-seq datasets from varied tissues, platforms, and protocols. Pretrained on over 1.97 million single cells from more than 30 organs or tissues and 6 major disease categories~(e.g., Alzheimer’s, Parkinson’s, leukemia, glioma), ChromFound leverages 1.86 trillion training tokens, surpassing the scale of existing models such as Geneformer\cite{geneformer}, scGPT\cite{cui2024scgpt}, and scFoundation\cite{hao2024large}, all of which use fewer than 1 trillion tokens.

% we introduce \textbf{ChromFound}, a universal \textbf{found}ation model for single-cell \textbf{chrom}atin accessibility data. It employs a hybrid architecture combining selective state-space models for efficient long-range sequence processing with self-attention blocks to capture local regulatory dependencies (±200 kb around TSS), aligned with enhancer-promoter interaction scales from Hi-C analysis \cite{Bonev2016OrganizationAF}.  Furthermore, we employ a biologically informed OCR tokenization to encode genomic coordinates and non-binary accessibility profiles. The unified tokenization strategy maintains scalability and facilitates alignment across heterogeneous datasets with inconsistent OCR definitions derived from diverse tissues, platforms, and experimental protocols. ChromFound is pre-trained in a large and diverse corpus of scATAC-seq data, comprising over 200 million single cells spanning more than 30 organs and tissue types and 7 major disease categories, including Alzheimer’s disease, Parkinson’s disease, leukemia and glioma, etc. The total number of training tokens exceeds 1.86 trillion, substantially exceeding the token counts used in Geneformer \cite{geneformer}, scGPT \cite{cui2024scgpt}, and scFoundation \cite{hao2024large}, which all use fewer than 1 trillion tokens.
To validate the effectiveness of ChromFound, we conduct comprehensive evaluations across multiple tasks and datasets. In cell representation, ChromFound outperforms baselines instead of additional training, achieving an average ARI improvement of 17.47\% across 8 scATAC-seq datasets from 4 tissues. Additional results demonstrate its robustness to denoise technical noise in zero-shot settings. For cell type annotation, ChromFound significantly improves accuracy and macro F1 score across all evaluated datasets and tissues. In cross-omics prediction, it accurately infers gene expression from chromatin accessibility, surpassing all baselines across 5 datasets. Furthermore, in the K562 cell line, ChromFound effectively identifies gene-enhancer relationships and regulatory perturbation effects, highlighting its potential for interpretable \textit{cis}-regulatory modeling.
% To validate the efficiency of ChromFound, we comprehensively evaluate ChromFound across multiple tasks and diversified datasets. In the cell clustering task, ChromFound demonstrates strong zero-shot performance in eight scATAC-seq datasets from four distinct tissues, outperforming all baseline methods requiring self-supervised training with an average 17.47\% gain in ARI metric. ChromFound also shows superior denoising and batch integration capabilities in zero-shot settings. For cell type annotation, ChromFound achieves significant improvements in all datasets and tissue types both in accuracy and macro F1 score. In cross-omics prediction tasks, ChromFound accurately infers gene expression from chromatin accessibility, consistently delivering performance gains across 5 datasets. Finally, in the K562 cell line, ChromFound significantly improves the prediction of gene-enhancer relationships and regulatory perturbation effects, underscoring its potential for interpretable cis-regulatory modeling.

Our main contributions can be summarized as follows:

(1) ChromFound, the first scATAC-seq foundation model, employs a genome-wide architecture along with a genome-aware tokenization and is pretrained on a large corpus with 1.97 million single cells.

(2) ChromFound provides a strong zero-shot cell representation from an epigenetic perspective, as well as showing consistent robustness to data sparsity and batch effect.

(3) ChromFound shows great transfer learning capabilities in cell annotation and cross-omics prediction, validating biological significance in inferring enhancer-gene links and perturbation responses.

\section{Related Work}
Single-cell foundation models for scRNA-seq data have seen significant advancements, supporting tasks such as gene function prediction, cell annotation, and drug response modeling. Geneformer \cite{geneformer}, scGPT \cite{cui2024scgpt}, and scFoundation \cite{hao2024large} are trained by large-scale datasets, while scBERT \cite{yang2022scbert} and CellPLM \cite{wen2023cellplm} focus on specific objectives based on pre-training language models such as imputation and cell communications. However, existing models are predominantly tailored for scRNA-seq data and fail to model scATAC-seq data effectively. GET \cite{fu2025foundation} achieves a level of predictive precision comparable to experimental replicates by integrating chromatin accessibility data and genomic sequence information in a pseudo-bulk format instead of single-cell level.

Deep learning methods are widely used in scATAC-seq data analysis for cell clustering and cell annotation. SCALE~\cite{xiong2019scale} and its extension SCALEX~\cite{xiong2022online} leverage the variational autoencoder~\cite{kingma2013auto} for latent feature extraction and multi-omics integration, while CASTLE~\cite{cui2024discrete} enhances cell type annotation through self-supervised learning. scBasset~\cite{yuan2022scbasset} connects the genomic sequence with binary accessibility and shows its strong ability to cell representation and batch integration. Cellcano~ \cite{ma2023cellcano} and SANGO~\cite{zeng2024deciphering} employ a two-step MLP for cell type annotation. 
% Similarly, EpiAnno~\cite{chen2022cell} integrates deep learning for the same task. 
These models are impeded by their fundamental architectural limitations and the scarcity of available data, thereby restricting their scalability and generalizability across diverse datasets.

