\section{Details of Datasets}
\label{datasets_details}
We collect a large-scale human scATAC-seq dataset that includes more than 2.65 million cells and 1.75 trillion tokens. ChromFound for pretraining is based on two fundamental datasets, the human atlas \cite{zhang2021single} and the fetal atlas \cite{domcke2020human}, which contribute 1.32 million cells spanning multiple human organs and serve as the primary sources for pretraining ChromFound. We accept the CRCh38 as reference genome across all datasets to maintain uniformity, converting datasets originally in hg19 to hg38 when necessary. The resources of all datasets are detailed in Table~\ref{tab:dataset_info}.

\section{Pre-processing of scATAC-seq Data}
\label{sec:data_processing}
We implement a pre-process pipeline to scATAC-seq data, addressing sparsity and dimensionality to ensure high-quality cells and OCRs for model pretraining and downstream tasks. Pre-processing is performed after the train/test splits in pretraining and downstream tasks, preventing any information leakage. All datasets are saved in \texttt{.h5ad} format with the OCR coordinates of genome in \texttt{var} schema. We detail the pre-processing methods in the following.

\textbf{Cell filtering}: We filter cells with non-zero counts lower than 1000 or higher than 60000 for every dataset.

\textbf{OCR filtering}: We compute the non-zero counts of each OCR by cell types and filter OCRs with non-zero counts lower than 5\% in all cell types, ensuring that long-tail cell types remain heterogeneous. 

\textbf{Normalization and Log Transformation}: To stabilize variance and scale the data, we apply total count normalization followed by log transformation.

\section{More related works about Mamba and hybrid architecture}
Mamba-based models, such as Mamba \cite{gu2023mamba} and Mamba-2 \cite{mamba-2}, replace self-attention with selective State Space Models (SSMs) \cite{lstm,gu2021combining,gu2021efficiently}, achieving linear complexity while maintaining strong performance. Recently, Mamba has been applied in biology and genomics such as RNA foundation models \cite{fradkin2024orthrus} and DNA sequence modeling \cite{schiff2024caduceus}, efficiently handling sparse and long-range dependencies in biological data. 

We have noticed that some works have started to use hybrid transformer-mamba structures for modeling time series and image data. After conducting some theoretical research and analysis, we found that the existing hybrid structures are not suitable for scATAC-seq modeling. Therefore, ChromFound has been meticulously designed for scATAC-seq data, with fundamental differences from existing hybrid structures. Below, we highlight two important papers for a detailed theoretical comparison.

1. The SST~\cite{xu2024sst} uses transformers and mamba to separately model short-range and long-range dependencies, then concatenates the features of short-range and long-range interactions. This resembles a parallel stacking structure. In contrast, ChromFound employs a sequential stacking approach, where data first passes through the transformer for short-range feature interactions and then moves to mamba for long-range learning.

2. MambaVision~\cite{hatamizadeh2024mambavision} integrates Vision Transformers with Mamba and is tailored for vision applications. ChromFound, however, has been designed specifically to accommodate the local strong interactions present in scATAC-seq data. We introduced a novel window partition self-attention module to better capture these interactions.

