\section{Discussion of comparing OCR tokenization with other peak-calling algorithms}
Below, we discuss about its experimental justification and practical utility compared to approaches relying on consistent peak-calling pipelines.

\textbf{1. Experimental validation and ablation study demonstrate the efficacy of genome-aware OCR tokenization}:
Our genome-aware tokenization framework integrates three biologically grounded components: chromosome identity, genomic coordinates, and accessibility values. By explicitly encoding positional and genomic context, ChromFound adapts to OCRs derived from heterogeneous peak-calling pipelines without requiring a unified feature space.
The ablation study in~\ref{ablation_study} empirically validates this design: the inclusion of genome-aware tokenization improves clustering performance compared to a baseline lacking genomic embeddings. 

\textbf{2. Genome-aware tokenization reduces preprocessing complexity}:
A critical advantage of ChromFound lies in its ability to operate directly on precomputed count matrices (e.g., .h5ad files), eliminating dependencies on raw fragment files or reprocessing through specific peak-calling pipelines. Publicly available scATAC-seq datasets often only provide processed matrices, rendering methods reliant on unified peak sets impractical. ChromFound circumvents these limitations, ensuring immediate applicability to datasets lacking raw sequencing data.
Even when fragment files are accessible, reprocessing data through unified pipelines (e.g., ENCODE) imposes prohibitive burdens. Such workflows require extensive data preprocessing, and substantial computational resources—particularly for large-scale datasets (>1 million cells). ChromFound bypasses these inefficiencies, enabling rapid integration of new data.

\textbf{3. Scalability of genome-aware tokenization}:
The variable OCR tokenization ensures robust scalability across datasets with divergent OCR definitions. When integrating datasets with minimal OCR overlap (e.g., novel tissues or rare cell types), fixed peak sets discard biologically meaningful OCRs unique to new data. ChromFound dynamically encodes OCRs based on genomic coordinates, preserving dataset-specific regulatory elements critical for discovery-driven tasks.

\textbf{Summary}:
ChromFound addresses two critical limitations of existing approaches: (1) dependency on raw fragment files and reprocessing workflows, and (2) inability to handle datasets with low OCR overlap. By genome-aware tokenization, our framework enables integration of heterogeneous scATAC-seq datasets without complex bioinformatic pipelines. This design aligns with the core objectives of foundation models—scalability, universality, and practical utility—as demonstrated by its state-of-the-art performance across diverse tasks.

\section{Analysis of Cell Type Annotation}
\label{analysis of celltype annotation}
\subsection{Metric Value of Cell Type Annotation}
\label{cell_type_annotation_appendix}
To adapt ChromFound for cell type annotation, we leverage its four-layer pretrained model, extracting the encoder output as a general-purpose representation. The encoder's hidden dimension is expanded from 128 to 256 and projected to a single dimension, forming a pooling tensor. This tensor is then processed by additional multilayer perceptron (MLP) layers to predict cell type logits. During fine-tuning, the pretrained decoder parameters are frozen, while gradients flow through the rest of the backbone to align with the classification objective. Dropout and LayerNorm are applied to mitigate overfitting and stabilize training, respectively. Benchmark methods are implemented using their default parameters as provided in their source codes.

For the experimental setup, we split the training data into 90\% for training and 10\% for validation. Training is conducted over 20 epochs using the AdamW optimizer with an initial learning rate of $5 \times 10^{-4}$ and a 50-step learning rate warmup schedule. The model is selected based on validation performance and evaluated on the test set to compute accuracy and macro F1-score. For certain configurations, such as training on EPF\_hydrop\_2 and testing on VIB\_10xv1\_1, the learning rate is reduced to $2.5 \times 10^{-4}$ to ensure stable convergence. All experiments are performed on a single machine with four NVIDIA A100 GPUs.

