We investigated two mechanisms that may explain these differences in student conceptual learning across methods: peer networks and classroom activities. To examine the types of peer networks that students develop, we created two undirected networks per course (one pre-semester and one post-semester) with nodes representing students and edges representing reported interactions between students. For each course, we applied a temporal exponential random graph model to measure whether different structures were more (or less) likely to form in the post-semester network than we would expect if the pre-semester network evolved randomly. We used separable temporal exponential random graph models in particular, as they isolate the process of edge formation (i.e., edges forming in the post-semester network that were not present in the pre-semester network)~\cite{krivitsky2014separable,hunter2008goodness}. We included four predictor variables in the model: edges (intercept term counting the total number of edges between students), chains (count of unclosed triangles of edges among three students, measuring string-like structures), triangles (count of closed triangles of edges among three students, measuring small group structures), and degree distribution (count of number of edges connected to each student; see Table~\ref{tab:tergmdefs} in End Matter and Supplemental Material~\cite{supplemental} for further details). 


Peer network development is comparable across all four active learning methods (signs and significance of most coefficients are similar for each variable in Fig.~\ref{fig:tergm}). In almost all courses, students were not more or less likely to develop string-like chains of connections to peers than we would expect at random (odds ratios close to one for chains) but were significantly likely to develop connections with small groups of peers (odds ratios greater than one for triangles). With the exception of Tutorials courses, students were likely to form connections with peers if the connections created a highly uniform degree distribution (i.e., with all students having a similar number of connections; odds ratios less than one for degree distribution). In Tutorials, the odds ratios for the degree distribution variable were not distinguishable from one in any course due to large standard errors on the estimates. The ways that students develop peer networks, therefore, likely do not explain the variation in conceptual learning gains observed in Fig.~\ref{fig:CI}.


We then examined possible variation in the activities that took place during instruction within each active learning method. We applied the Classroom Observation Protocol for Undergraduate STEM (COPUS)~\cite{smith2013classroom,landis1977measurement} to 114 h of classroom video collected across 90 observations of 30 courses in our study (three observations per course). The COPUS records the co-occurrence of certain behaviors of the instructor (e.g., lecturing, asking a clicker question, posing a question, and guiding groupwork) and the students (e.g., asking a question, answering a clicker question in groups, completing a worksheet in groups, and completing other forms of groupwork) at two-minute intervals (see Table~\ref{tab:observationinfo} in End Matter). We combined our 90 COPUS observations with 133 comparable COPUS observations from two prior studies of introductory physics courses (to reach a sufficient sample size for reliable results)~\cite{commeford2022characterizing,stains2018anatomy} in a Latent Profile Analysis to identify groups of similar observations, or ``profiles" (see Supplemental Material~\cite{supplemental} for further details)~\cite{masyn2013latent-key,nylund2018ten,howard2018variable,hipp2006local,hallquist2018mplusautomation}. For the 90 observations from our study, we examined how the observations from each active learning method were distributed across the identified profiles. 


Four profiles of classroom activities emerged from our analysis: (1) ``Lecture," where the instructor lectures for most of the class time (blue dots in Fig.~\ref{fig:profiles}a), (2) ``Clickers," where lecture is supplemented with group clicker questions (orange dots in Fig.~\ref{fig:profiles}a), (3) ``Worksheets," where most of the class time is spent on group worksheets (yellow dots in Fig.~\ref{fig:profiles}a), and (4) ``Other Groupwork," where most of the class time is spent on other types of groupwork activities (e.g., solving problems on whiteboards and completing laboratory activities; green dots in Fig.~\ref{fig:profiles}a). For the 90 observations from our study, a slight majority of ISLE observations belong to the Lecture profile, most Peer Instruction observations belong to the Clickers profile, most Tutorials observations belong to the Worksheets profile, and most SCALE-UP observations belong to the Other Groupwork profile (Fig~\ref{fig:profiles}b). This means there are differences in the instructional practices used in each of the four analyzed active learning methods. 

For observations in both the Worksheets and Other Groupwork profiles (i.e., mostly Tutorials and SCALE-UP courses), more than 70\% of class time was spent on student-centered activities (worksheets or other kinds of groupwork, respectively; Fig.~\ref{fig:profiles}a). For observations in the Lecture and Clickers profiles (i.e., mostly ISLE and Peer Instruction courses), less than half of class time was spent on student-centered activities. The differences in conceptual learning observed in Fig.~\ref{fig:CI}, therefore, may be (at least partly) attributable to differences in the fraction of class time spent on student-centered activities. %SCALE-UP courses, where about three-quarters of class time was devoted to student-centered activities, exhibited significantly higher learning gains than ISLE and Peer Instruction courses, where instructors lectured for at least half of the class time.

%Interestingly, but likely not to differences in the particular types of student-centered activities (i.e., Tutorials and SCALE-UP use different activities for a similar amount of class time and have similar learning gains in Fig.~\ref{fig:CI}). SCALE-UP courses, where about three-quarters of class time was devoted to student-centered activities, exhibited significantly higher learning gains than ISLE and Peer Instruction courses, where instructors lectured for at least half of the class time.


\textit{Discussion--}In response to explicit calls for researchers to stop comparing active learning to traditional teaching methods and to instead compare different forms of active learning~\cite{dancy2024physics,wieman2014large}, we measured the relative impacts of four established active learning methods in introductory physics and astronomy: ISLE, Peer Instruction, Tutorials, and SCALE-UP. Using data from 28 different institutions, we found significant increases in student conceptual learning in all four methods, ranging from 2.09$\sigma$ to 6.22$\sigma$ differences from a null effect. 

We also found SCALE-UP to be associated with significantly larger conceptual learning gains than both ISLE (2.25$\sigma$ difference) and Peer Instruction (2.54$\sigma$ difference). Surprisingly, these differences do not seem attributable to variation in the ways that students develop peer networks in each active learning method. Rather, the differences are (at least partly) related to variation in the amount of class time spent on student-centered activities. SCALE-UP courses spent the majority of class time on groupwork activities such as laboratory activities or problem solving at whiteboards, while instructors lectured for a large fraction of class time in many ISLE and Peer Instruction courses. Devoting more class time to student-centered activities likely provides students with more opportunities to construct their understanding in collaboration with their peers, enhancing their conceptual learning~\cite{piaget1926language,vygotsky1978mind}. 

A similar positive correlation between class time spent on student-centered activities and student conceptual learning has been reported across different implementations of Tutorials in introductory astronomy courses~\cite{prather2009national} and in biology courses~\cite{weir2019small}. In biology contexts, researchers also found that worksheets were particularly effective for student conceptual learning~\cite{weir2019small}. Our data, however, suggest that worksheets and other types of groupwork have comparable effects in the discipline of physics: Tutorials and SCALE-UP courses used different types of groupwork (Fig.~\ref{fig:profiles}b) but exhibited similar conceptual learning gains (Fig.~\ref{fig:CI}). Future research experiments should collect similar data from a broader set of active learning physics courses (i.e., including courses using any form of active learning, not only a named active learning method) and compare conceptual learning gains across latent profiles of instructional practices to identify causal mechanisms of conceptual learning.

In the remainder of this section, we explore other possible explanations for differences in conceptual learning between SCALE-UP, ISLE, and Peer Instruction and, where relevant, suggest avenues for future research studies. First, the differences could be due to variation in the concept inventories that were used across courses, as we allowed instructors to decide which assessment was most relevant to their course. The Force Concept Inventory, however, was the most commonly used assessment across all active learning methods (see Table~\ref{tab:CIinfo} in End Matter). Our measurements of conceptual learning, therefore, are comparable across active learning methods.

We also studied both physics and astronomy courses, and it is possible that active learning methods are more beneficial in one discipline than the other. The two astronomy courses included in our analysis of conceptual learning, however, were taught using Tutorials and these two courses exhibited comparable conceptual learning gains to the other Tutorials courses, ruling out this possibility.

%The active learning methods with significantly different impacts on conceptual learning (SCALE-UP, ISLE, and Peer Instruction), however, were all physics (not astronomy) courses, ruling out this possibility (see Table~\ref{tab:CIinfo} in End Matter). The two astronomy courses included in our analysis of conceptual learning were taught using Tutorials.

Variation in class size may also explain the relative benefits of active learning methods to conceptual learning. Prior research in other disciplines has found larger learning gains in small-enrollment active learning courses than large-enrollment active learning courses~\cite{murdoch2002active}. In this study, however, the ISLE, Tutorials, and SCALE-UP courses had similar class sizes (Peer Instruction courses were larger, on average, than these three methods; see Table~\ref{tab:classsize} in End Matter), eliminating this explanation of our results. 

Relatedly, the physical configuration of  active learning classrooms (e.g., tables set up for group work versus large lecture halls) could impact conceptual learning. In our study, however, a few of the ISLE and Peer Instruction courses took place entirely in classrooms with small tables for group work (similar to the SCALE-UP layout) rather than lecture halls. A prior study comparing two identically-taught introductory biology courses, one held in a SCALE-UP classroom and one held in a traditional lecture hall, also found that student learning did not vary across layouts~\cite{stoltzfus2016does}.  Our observed differences in conceptual learning gains between SCALE-UP, ISLE, and Peer Instruction, therefore, are likely not attributable to different physical classroom layouts.

Another possibility is that, as prior research suggests, there is significant variation in instructor adaptation of established active learning methods~\cite{turpen2009not,wood2016characterizing} and that without proper support these adaptations may not produce as much conceptual learning gains as high fidelity implementations~\cite{andrews2011active}. We found that all four active learning methods analyzed here exhibited similar extents of variation in implementation (Fig.~\ref{fig:profiles}b). The ISLE observations in particular spanned the Lecture and Worksheets profiles, although the developers intended for this method to integrate lecture, laboratory, and other activities together in one course component~\cite{etkina2007investigative}. The ISLE instructors in our study likely adopted the ISLE method for the laboratory component of their course, but left the lecture section unchanged. %ISLE also had the most variability in conceptual learning gains, with the largest confidence interval in Fig.~\ref{fig:CI}. 
Future research should disentangle the effects of different implementations of ISLE (and other active learning methods) by directly measuring fidelity of implementation (e.g., by analyzing the quality of classroom activities) and relating this fidelity to student outcomes.

%active learning methods vary in the extent to which they are ``prescribed." SCALE-UP, for example, mostly provides guidelines for the physical classroom layout, while ISLE is both a learning philosophy and a written curriculum of specific classroom activities. It could be that less prescribed methods, such as SCALE-UP, are associated with larger learning gains if instructors are able to more heavily adapt these methods to fit their local context (e.g., student population). 


The goals of different active learning methods may also explain the variation in conceptual learning that we found in this study. ISLE, for example, focuses on engaging students in scientific processes (e.g., through conducting experiments) in addition to improving their content knowledge. Prior research has demonstrated that ISLE does effectively improve student engagement in scientific processes~\cite{etkina2010design}, however other work has found that physics laboratories often do not contribute to student conceptual understanding~\cite{holmes2017value}. Future work should measure multiple different student outcomes (e.g., scientific processes, critical thinking skills, and conceptual understanding) to understand the full realm of benefits of each active learning method to students.

Finally, we note that many instructors likely blend together different active learning methods to meet the needs of their own instructional environment. In this study, we collected data from instructors who reported only using one of the four established methods we aimed to analyze. Similar studies that contain a broader sample of active learning courses and instructors (e. g., including those outside of the United States) will validate and extend our findings. We also recommend for researchers to conduct similar studies in other scientific disciplines (e.g., biology and engineering) and in courses beyond the introductory level.


